SPORT_EXPLAINERS += [

("how-football-agents-get-paid", "How football agents actually get paid",
"The percentages, the caps, the licences and the &ldquo;client pays&rdquo; rule: the money machine inside every transfer, explained from the regulations.",
"""<p>Every transfer story has a hidden character: the agent. They negotiate the deal, but how they are paid &mdash; by whom, how much, and under what licence &mdash; is governed by rules most fans have never seen. Since January 2023 those rules have had a name: the FIFA Football Agent Regulations (FFAR).</p>
<h2>The old world: fees without a ceiling</h2>
<p>Under the previous intermediary system (2015&ndash;2023), registration was light and fee recommendations were non-binding. In practice, agents' commissions on the biggest deals ran into the millions, usually paid by the buying club on the player's behalf &mdash; an arrangement critics called a structural conflict of interest, because the agent negotiating the player's contract was paid by the other side of the table.</p>
<h2>The caps, article by article</h2>
<p>The FFAR's article 15 sets maximum service fees by who the agent represents and how much the player earns. The shape of it, per the regulations and law-firm summaries of them:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Representing the player or the engaging club:</b> up to 5% of the player's remuneration below the USD 200,000 threshold, and up to 3% above it &mdash; the two bands stack, so on a USD 1m salary the cap is 5% of the first 200k plus 3% of the rest.</li>
<li><b>Permitted dual representation</b> (agent acting for player and engaging club): up to 10% below the threshold, 6% above.</li>
<li><b>Representing the selling club:</b> up to 10% of the transfer compensation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two design details matter. Conditional payments count towards the cap calculation. And an engaging club may pay no more than half of the total commission due.</p>
<h2>&ldquo;Client pays&rdquo; &mdash; the structural change</h2>
<p>The deeper reform: players above the USD 200,000 remuneration threshold must pay their own agent. The logic is conflict-of-interest hygiene &mdash; the person negotiating your contract should be paid by you, not by the club across the table. In practice it moved real money from club accounts into player expenses, and it remains one of the most contested provisions in European courts.</p>
<h2>Licences and the Agents Chamber</h2>
<p>The FFAR also re-licensed the profession: agents must pass a FIFA exam and hold an annual licence, and disputes over representation agreements go to a dedicated FIFA Agents Chamber rather than ordinary courts. Whether every piece survives legal challenge is an open question &mdash; parts have been litigated since day one &mdash; but the architecture (caps, exams, client-pays, a chamber) is the system the market now operates inside.</p>
<p>The honest summary for fans: when a transfer is reported at &ldquo;&pound;80m plus add-ons&rdquo;, the agent fees attached to it are regulated percentages of salary and fee, not free numbers &mdash; and knowing who pays them explains half of the window's behaviour.</p>""",
[("Lewis Silkin — the new FIFA football agent regulations", "https://www.lewissilkin.com/insights/2023-01-06/the-new-fifa-football-agent-regulations-what-you-need-to-know"),
 ("Red Star Education — new FIFA Football Agent Regulations", "https://www.redstareducation.co.uk/blog/new-fifa-football-agent-regulations/")],
[("why-football-transfers-collapse", "Why transfers collapse"),
 ("what-does-a-sporting-director-do", "What a sporting director does"),
 ("how-football-contracts-work", "How football contracts work")]),

("how-transfer-fee-amortisation-works", "Why a &pound;100m signing does not cost &pound;100m this year",
"Transfer fees are spread across contracts in club accounts. Amortisation, explained: the yearly cost, the profit on sale, and why it moves PSR.",
"""<p>When a club pays &pound;100m for a player on a five-year contract, almost no club account shows a &pound;100m cost that year. The fee is spread across the contract &mdash; an accounting mechanism called <b>amortisation</b> &mdash; and understanding it explains a surprising share of transfer-market behaviour.</p>
<h2>The mechanism in one formula</h2>
<p>Annual amortisation = transfer fee &divide; contract length. Our &pound;100m signing on five years costs the accounts <b>&pound;20m a year</b>, every year, for five years. The player's wage sits on top of that, separately. The club paid the cash in instalments or not; the accounting cost is the same straight line.</p>
<h2>The book value &mdash; and the profit trick</h2>
<p>Each year the unspent remainder is the player's <b>net book value</b>: after two years of our example, it is &pound;60m. Now the club sells him for &pound;75m. The accounts record a <b>profit of &pound;15m</b> (sale price minus book value) &mdash; in the year of sale. This is why clubs can report big transfer profits in a single window: selling players late in their contracts converts small book values into large accounting gains.</p>
<p>It also explains the academy asymmetry: a homegrown player was never bought, so his book value is near zero, and <em>any</em> sale fee lands almost entirely as accounting profit. The classic deadline-day move &mdash; selling a graduate to balance the books &mdash; is this mechanic wearing a shirt.</p>
<h2>Why PSR makes it strategic, not cosmetic</h2>
<p>The Premier League's Profitability and Sustainability Rules assess clubs on aggregate adjusted losses over a rolling three-season period &mdash; historically a &pound;105m threshold before permitted add-backs and owner equity, per the League's own published guidance on PSR calculations. Amortised transfer cost counts against that; amortisation is therefore <em>spending control with a delay</em>: a longer contract lowers the yearly charge, which is why long deals became a financial instrument as much as a loyalty signal.</p>
<h2>The honest caveats</h2>
<p>Amortisation is accounting, not cash: fee payment schedules are negotiated separately, and cash flow can diverge from the straight line. Rules evolve &mdash; squad-cost ratios and new spending controls have been layered on since the PSR cases of 2024 &mdash; and different competitions (UEFA's rules, other leagues) count differently. But the core mechanic never changes: <b>fee &divide; years = the yearly number that moves everything else</b>. The desk's transfer amortisation calculator runs exactly this arithmetic, with the working shown.</p>""",
[("Premier League — statement on Profitability and Sustainability Rules", "https://www.premierleague.com/en/news/3938339"),
 ("Sporting News — what are Premier League Profit and Sustainability Rules?", "https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/premier-league-profit-sustainability-rules-financial-fair-play/42ef3c1a91043e191482250d")],
[("financial-fair-play-explained", "Financial Fair Play and PSR, explained"),
 ("premier-league-prize-money-explained", "Premier League prize money, explained"),
 ("how-do-football-clubs-make-money", "How football clubs make money")]),

("how-the-fifa-world-ranking-works", "How the FIFA world ranking actually works",
"Elo maths with an importance dial: the formula behind every &lsquo;ranked No.1&rsquo; headline, the numbers that move it, and what it is actually used for.",
"""<p>Every World Cup draw ceremony leans on one list: the FIFA world ranking. It looks like a simple ladder, but since August 2018 it has been a piece of borrowed chess mathematics &mdash; an Elo-style system where every international match moves both teams' points in opposite directions.</p>
<h2>The formula</h2>
<p>After each match, a team's points are updated as: <b>P = P(before) + I &times; (W &minus; W(e))</b>, where W is the result (1 for a win, 0.5 for a draw, 0 for a loss) and W(e) is the <em>expected</em> result given the two teams' current ratings: <b>W(e) = 1 / (10<sup>&minus;&Delta;/600</sup> + 1)</b>, with &Delta; the points gap between the sides. The elegance is in the expectation term: beating a much stronger team moves you a long way; beating a weak one barely moves you; losing to a giant costs you almost nothing.</p>
<h2>The importance dial</h2>
<p>I scales everything by what was at stake. The published scale runs from 5 (friendlies outside the international calendar) through 10 (calendar-window friendlies) and 15&ndash;25 (Nations League and qualifiers) to the top tier &mdash; around 35 for early-round matches of confederation finals, rising to 50&ndash;60 for World Cup knockout football. One World Cup semi-final swing can outweigh a year of friendlies by design.</p>
<h2>Two rules that surprise people</h2>
<p>First, <b>knockout-stage losses do not deduct points</b> in final competitions &mdash; losing a World Cup quarter-final leaves your rating untouched, so teams are never punished for reaching deep stages. Second, there is no home/away weighting and no goal-margin bonus; since 2021 points are carried to two decimal places, so the order can turn on hundredths.</p>
<h2>What it is for &mdash; and what it is not</h2>
<p>The ranking's real power is procedural: it seeds World Cup draws and qualification pots, decides which teams host or travel, and settles tie-breakers. It is not a prediction engine: it measures results weighted by context, not underlying quality, and a team coasting through friendly wins can outrank one that drew harder fixtures. When a headline says &ldquo;ranked No.1 in the world&rdquo;, the honest translation is: <em>best results, appropriately weighted, over the recent window</em> &mdash; which is a real achievement, just a narrower one than it sounds.</p>""",
[("FIFA — FIFA/Coca-Cola Men's World Ranking", "https://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/men"),
 ("Wikipedia — FIFA Men's World Ranking (calculation method)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_Men%27s_World_Ranking")],
[("how-the-2026-world-cup-format-works", "The 48-team World Cup, explained"),
 ("how-extra-time-and-penalty-shootouts-work", "Extra time and shootouts, explained")]),

("how-the-2026-world-cup-format-works", "The 48-team World Cup, explained",
"Twelve groups of four, a round of 32, 104 matches across three host countries: the 2026 format and what changed from the tournaments you remember.",
"""<p>The 2026 World Cup in the United States, Canada and Mexico is the first with <b>48 teams</b> &mdash; up from 32 &mdash; and the format changed with the numbers. Here is the shape of it, and the honest consequences.</p>
<h2>The structure</h2>
<p>Forty-eight teams, <b>twelve groups of four</b>. The group stage is a single round-robin: three matches each, top two from every group advance (24), plus the <b>eight best third-placed teams</b> &mdash; 32 survivors into a new <b>round of 32</b>, then the familiar straight knockout to the final. The total climbs to <b>104 matches</b>, the most in the tournament's history, played across 16 venues in three countries.</p>
<h2>Why it grew</h2>
<p>Expansion was a promise about access: more confederation slots mean more nations experiencing a World Cup &mdash; several 2026 debutants qualified through paths that simply did not exist at 32 teams. The commercial logic sits alongside the sporting one, and pretending otherwise is not honest: more teams, more matches, more markets, more broadcast inventory.</p>
<h2>What the new format does to the football</h2>
<p>Two effects matter on the pitch. First, the eight best third-placed places change group-stage arithmetic: four points &mdash; sometimes three &mdash; can now be enough to survive, so the final round of dead-looking group games matters more than ever. Second, the extra knockout round means a champion plays <b>eight matches</b>, not seven; squad depth and recovery science are now tournament strategy, not background noise.</p>
<h2>The three-host wrinkle</h2>
<p>Three countries means three time zones of travel, four if the west coast swings &mdash; and a scheduling problem nobody has solved before at this scale. The final lands at MetLife Stadium in New York/New Jersey; the opening match in Mexico City's Estadio Azteca, the first stadium to host three World Cups. Compare the shape to club football's own expansion &mdash; the Champions League's 36-team league phase &mdash; and the pattern is clear: the sport's big tournaments are trading the tidy formats of the past century for volume, and both changes argue about the same thing: what a tournament is for.</p>""",
[("FIFA — FIFA World Cup 2026 (official tournament hub)", "https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026"),
 ("Wikipedia — 2026 FIFA World Cup", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_FIFA_World_Cup")],
[("how-the-fifa-world-ranking-works", "How the FIFA world ranking works"),
 ("how-the-champions-league-works", "How the Champions League works")]),

("how-goal-line-technology-works", "How goal-line technology actually works",
"Cameras or magnetic fields, a one-second answer, a vibrating watch: the binary piece of tech football accepted instantly &mdash; and why VAR still divides.",
"""<p>Goal-line technology (GLT) answers football's simplest and most furious question &mdash; <em>did the whole ball cross the line?</em> &mdash; in under a second, to the referee alone. Approved by the IFAB in July 2012 after decades of resistance, it is the one piece of match technology almost nobody argues with. Understanding why takes about two minutes.</p>
<h2>Two competing engineering answers</h2>
<p>Certified systems have used two approaches. <b>Camera-based systems</b> track the ball with multiple high-speed cameras per goal and triangulate its position against the line to millimetre accuracy. <b>Magnetic-field systems</b> buried cables around the goal and sensed a chipped ball crossing the field. Both must pass the FIFA Quality Programme certification and independent testing before a competition may use them; the law cares about the guarantee, not the method.</p>
<h2>The rules that make it boring &mdash; in the best way</h2>
<p>The Laws of the Game impose a deliberately tight box, per IFAB Law 1's GLT provisions: the system applies <b>solely to the goal line</b>, is used <b>only to determine whether a goal was scored</b>, must confirm <b>immediately &mdash; within one second</b> &mdash; and communicates <b>only to the match officials</b> (vibration and a visual signal on the referee's watch, optionally to the video operation room). Before every match the referee must test the system; if it fails the test, it must not be used. Binary question, binary answer, single recipient.</p>
<h2>Why it settled and VAR did not</h2>
<p>The contrast is instructive. GLT answers a question with one correct answer that physics can settle &mdash; ball over line or not. VAR answers questions with <em>judgement</em> in them: was it clear and obvious? was that enough contact? was the attacker involved? Nobody disputes the goal-line computer because there is nothing to dispute; the arguments around VAR are arguments about human interpretation wearing a technology costume. GLT also never interrupts the game: the answer arrives on a wrist mid-play, and unless the referee signals a goal, most fans never know the system spoke.</p>
<h2>The history in one line each</h2>
<p>The pressure built through a decade of howlers &mdash; most famously the 2010 World Cup disallowed goal that crossed by half a metre. The IFAB voted unanimously in July 2012; the Premier League adopted it for 2013-14; it is now standard at the top of the game and, like semi-automated offside after it, is gradually filtering down as costs fall. The desk's VAR explainer covers the slower, messier sibling; the goal-line machine, happily, has nothing left to explain &mdash; which is the highest praise football technology gets.</p>""",
[("IFAB — Laws of the Game, Law 1: The Field of Play (GLT provisions)", "https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/the-field-of-play/"),
 ("Wikipedia — Goal-line technology", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goal-line_technology")],
[("how-var-works", "How VAR actually works"),
 ("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained")]),

("how-football-contracts-work", "How football contracts actually work",
"Length limits, the protected period, just cause and the clauses inside the clauses: the legal skeleton under every transfer headline, from FIFA's rulebook.",
"""<p>Behind every transfer rumour is a document: the player's employment contract. Football's global rulebook &mdash; FIFA's Regulations on the Status and Transfer of Players (RSTP) &mdash; sets the frame, and national leagues fill it in. The mechanics are less mysterious than the headlines.</p>
<h2>Length: one year minimum, five maximum</h2>
<p>The RSTP sets the boundaries: a professional contract must run at least from its effective date to the end of the season, and <b>no longer than five years</b> (exceptions require league approval). Everything else &mdash; wages, image rights, bonuses &mdash; is negotiation inside that frame. Contract length became a financial instrument when amortisation entered the picture: the same fee spread over five years costs the accounts a fifth as much per season as over one, so long contracts are as much accounting strategy as commitment.</p>
<h2>The protected period &mdash; and why &lsquo;running it down&rsquo; works</h2>
<p>Football does not allow ordinary contract-breaking, but it does price it. The RSTP's <b>contractual stability</b> rules say a contract may only be terminated by mutual agreement or for <b>just cause</b>; outside a &ldquo;protected period&rdquo; (broadly three seasons or three years for deals signed before age 28, two for later signings), terminating without just cause means <b>compensation</b> &mdash; and sometimes sporting sanctions &mdash; rather than simple freedom. This is why the final year of a contract is leverage: with twelve months left, the selling club's negotiating asset is evaporating, and &ldquo;running down his deal&rdquo; is a rational strategy with a price tag attached.</p>
<h2>The clauses inside the clauses</h2>
<p>Release clauses (a fixed price a buying club can trigger &mdash; the desk explains them separately), sell-on percentages owed to former clubs, loyalty bonuses, and appearance add-ons all live inside or beside the contract. Since the 2023 agent regulations, representation costs attach to contracts too: agents for higher-earning players are paid by the player, with capped percentages &mdash; a detail that changes who really pays in a negotiation.</p>
<h2>What actually ends contracts</h2>
<p>Mutual termination, expiry, just cause (unpaid wages being the classic player-side trigger), or the sporting-just-cause routes for players frozen out of squads. The transfer itself is a fourth thing entirely: two clubs agreeing a fee while the player agrees new personal terms &mdash; two documents, both required, which is exactly where &ldquo;done deals&rdquo; collapse. When you read &ldquo;in talks&rdquo;, the honest translation is: <em>a contract is being written</em>, and every clause above is a line of it.</p>""",
[("FIFA — Regulations on the Status and Transfer of Players (official PDF)", "https://digitalhub.fifa.com/m/19e76b9a9a9ae0ce/original/adi1292xtnibmwrqnimy-pdf.pdf"),
 ("Lewis Silkin — the new FIFA football agent regulations", "https://www.lewissilkin.com/insights/2023-01-06/the-new-fifa-football-agent-regulations-what-you-need-to-know")],
[("what-is-a-release-clause", "What a release clause is"),
 ("how-football-agents-get-paid", "How football agents get paid"),
 ("why-football-transfers-collapse", "Why transfers collapse")]),
]

# ---- Growth batch 2 (2026-09-25): competitions, technology and development ----
# Evergreen mechanics explainers, second wave. Same house rules: no betting,
# no odds, no tips; formats dated honestly; every claim sourced.

SPORT_EXPLAINERS += [

("how-the-ballon-dor-is-decided", "How the Ballon d'Or is actually decided",
"Thirty nominees, one hundred journalists, three criteria and a points ladder: the machinery behind football's most argued individual prize.",
"""<p>Every autumn, football argues about the Ballon d'Or as if it were a verdict. It is not a verdict &mdash; it is a vote, with a defined electorate, a defined ballot and defined criteria. Knowing the machine does not end the argument, but it tells you exactly what kind of claim the result can support.</p>
<h2>The electorate: one journalist per country</h2>
<p>The award is presented by France Football, and since the 2022 reform the voting body is deliberately narrow: <b>one journalist from each of the top 100 FIFA-ranked nations</b> votes in the men's award (the women's Ballon d'Or uses 50 journalists from the top 50 ranked nations). Not players, not coaches, not fans &mdash; a press jury, one voice per nation, weighted by nothing but its own ballot.</p>
<h2>The ballot: a ranked top ten</h2>
<p>The editorial teams of France Football and L'&Eacute;quipe assemble a <b>30-player shortlist</b> from the previous season's performances. Each juror then ranks their <b>top ten in order</b>, and the positions carry points on a descending ladder &mdash; 15 for first, then 12, 10, 8, 7, 5, 4, 3, 2 and 1. Most points wins. The shape of this ladder matters: a player who is everyone's second choice can lose to one who is a plurality's first choice, which is why &ldquo;best season&rdquo; and &ldquo;wins the Ballon d'Or&rdquo; are not synonyms.</p>
<h2>The three criteria &mdash; and the one that decides it</h2>
<p>Jurors are instructed to weigh three things: <b>individual performance</b> (decisive and impressive), <b>team achievements</b> (trophies won), and <b>class and fair play</b>. In practice, the second criterion does the heavy lifting: in a World Cup or Champions League year, the trophy almost always anchors the winner's case, because a hundred journalists from a hundred countries converge most reliably on silverware. Since 2022 the award also runs on a <b>season</b> (roughly August to July), not a calendar year &mdash; a quiet but real change in what the prize measures.</p>
<h2>The satellite trophies</h2>
<p>The same gala distributes the related awards, each with its own jury: the <b>Kopa Trophy</b> for the best under-21 (voted by former Ballon d'Or winners), the <b>Yashin Trophy</b> for the best goalkeeper, and the women's awards. Different electorates, different shortlists &mdash; worth remembering when a result in one category surprises you.</p>
<h2>What the prize can and cannot claim</h2>
<p>The honest framing: the Ballon d'Or is the aggregated opinion of a hundred journalists about a season, filtered through a points ladder and anchored by trophies. It is a legitimate record of consensus &mdash; and consensus is exactly what it is. Anyone presenting it as an objective measurement of the world's best footballer is selling certainty the ballot never promised. The desk's living 2026 Ballon d'Or question applies precisely this reading.</p>""",
[("BBC Sport — how the Ballon d'Or winner is decided", "https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c4g7jynnydlo"),
 ("beIN Sports — how the Ballon d'Or is decided: rules, voting and jury", "https://www.beinsports.com/en-us/soccer/articles/how-the-2025-ballon-d-or-is-decided-rules-voting-and-who-makes-up-the-jury-2025-09-22")],
[("how-the-fifa-world-ranking-works", "How the FIFA world ranking works"),
 ("best-football-players-in-the-world-2026", "Best football players in the world 2026")]),

("how-the-nations-league-works", "How the UEFA Nations League works",
"Leagues instead of friendlies, promotion and relegation for countries, and a trophy in June: the competition that reorganised the international calendar.",
"""<p>The Nations League exists because nobody liked international friendlies &mdash; not fans, not broadcasters, not the players. UEFA's answer, launched in 2018, was to turn the empty calendar slots into a league system with the one mechanic football already loved at club level: <b>promotion and relegation between divisions</b>.</p>
<h2>The pyramid of leagues</h2>
<p>UEFA's members are sorted into leagues by strength &mdash; currently four (A, B, C, D), with Leagues A, B and C holding 16 teams each in four groups of four, and a small League D. Allocation follows the previous edition's overall ranking, so the system is self-sorting: win and you rise, lose and you fall. Within each group, teams play a home-and-away round robin across the September, October and November international windows.</p>
<h2>What each league plays for</h2>
<p>In <b>League A</b>, the group winners and runners-up (eight teams) advance to two-legged quarter-finals in March, and the four winners meet in a June Finals &mdash; semi-finals, a final, a trophy. In every league, group winners are promoted and bottom teams relegated (movement between adjacent leagues can involve play-offs). The stakes convert meaningless autumn fixtures into matches that change a country's competitive future, which was the entire point.</p>
<h2>The qualification backdoor</h2>
<p>The second design goal: give the Euros and World Cup qualifying a safety net. Nations League performance feeds play-off places for the major tournaments, so a strong league campaign can rescue a poor qualifying group &mdash; and several recent tournament slots have been decided this way. It makes the autumn window matter twice.</p>
<h2>What changes next</h2>
<p>The competition keeps evolving, and the direction is consolidation: UEFA has approved a move, from the 2028/29 edition, to <b>three leagues of 18 teams</b> each &mdash; three groups of six playing six matches &mdash; with European Qualifiers restructured into tiers alongside it. Formats are moving parts here, so treat any single-season description (including this one) as dated the moment you read it; the durable ideas are the three: leagues by strength, movement between them, and a finals weekend that crowns a champion from what used to be the dead weeks of the calendar.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia — 2026–27 UEFA Nations League (format)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026%E2%80%9327_UEFA_Nations_League"),
 ("Wikipedia — UEFA Nations League", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UEFA_Nations_League")],
[("how-the-fifa-world-ranking-works", "How the FIFA world ranking works"),
 ("promotion-and-relegation-explained", "Promotion and relegation, explained")]),

("how-the-club-world-cup-works", "How the Club World Cup works",
"From a seven-team appendix to a 32-team summer World Cup every four years: the format, the qualification maths and why clubs argued about it.",
"""<p>For two decades the Club World Cup was a curiosity: seven continental champions, one week in December, a European winner nearly every time. In 2025 FIFA rebuilt it into something genuinely different &mdash; a <b>32-team tournament every four years</b>, played across a month of summer, modelled deliberately on the international World Cup. The revamp is as significant as it is contested, and understanding the format explains both halves of that sentence.</p>
<h2>The shape of the tournament</h2>
<p>Thirty-two clubs, <b>eight groups of four</b>, single round-robin group stage; the top two from each group advance to a <b>straight knockout</b> &mdash; round of 16, quarter-finals, semi-finals, final &mdash; with extra time and penalties for ties and no third-place play-off. It is the World Cup template applied to clubs, and that comparison is the point FIFA was making.</p>
<h2>Who gets in: the allocation maths</h2>
<p>The 32 places are split by confederation strength: <b>12 for Europe</b>, six for South America, four each for Africa, Asia and CONCACAF, one for Oceania, plus the host-nation slot. Within UEFA, places went to Champions League winners of the qualification period plus the clubs with the best cumulative four-year coefficient &mdash; so entry rewards sustained European performance, not a single lucky cup run. The same four-year ranking logic runs across the other confederations.</p>
<h2>The money, and the argument</h2>
<p>The commercial scale explains the controversy: the 2025 edition carried a prize pot reported around <b>&pound;775m</b>, with the winners earning up to roughly &pound;97m &mdash; money that dwarfs the old December version and rivals the Champions League itself. The objections arrived on schedule: player unions over calendar congestion in an already packed summer; clubs outside the qualified elite over the closed-ish door (qualification is by past performance, which entrenches the rich); and rival competition organisers over territory. All three objections are about the same thing &mdash; a four-year, month-long club mega-event has to take its weeks from somewhere.</p>
<h2>What it means on the pitch</h2>
<p>Practically: the best European sides now have a genuine world title to play for, South American clubs get a real competitive (if financially lopsided) shot at them, and the rest of the confederations get fixtures against the elite they rarely see. Whether the tournament settles into the calendar or becomes the football version of a bridge too far is the live question &mdash; the format answers how it works; the next edition will answer whether it should.</p>""",
[("BBC Sport — Club World Cup explained: teams, format, prize money", "https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c9835np3205o"),
 ("FIFA — FIFA Club World Cup 2025 (official tournament hub)", "https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/clubworldcup/25usa")],
[("how-the-2026-world-cup-format-works", "The 48-team World Cup, explained"),
 ("champions-league-new-format-explained", "The new Champions League format")]),

("how-semi-automated-offside-works", "How semi-automated offside actually works",
"Twelve cameras, 29 body points, a sensor in the ball: the limb-tracking behind the 3D animations &mdash; and why a human still decides.",
"""<p>The 3D animation that settles a tight offside looks like a video-game replay. It is actually the visible end of one of the densest tracking systems in sport &mdash; and the most misunderstood word in its name is <em>automated</em>. The system is <b>semi</b>-automated by design: it proposes, a human disposes.</p>
<h2>The tracking layer</h2>
<p>Per FIFA's technical documentation, the system combines two data streams. <b>Twelve dedicated tracking cameras</b> mounted under the stadium roof follow <b>up to 29 data points on each player</b> &mdash; every limb and extremity that matters for an offside call &mdash; <b>50 times per second</b>. Separately, a <b>sensor inside the ball</b> pinpoints the exact moment it is struck, solving the oldest hard problem of offside: knowing precisely when the pass was played.</p>
<h2>The decision layer &mdash; where the human stays</h2>
<p>An AI fuses the two streams and, when an attacker receives the ball in an offside position at the kick point, sends an <b>alert to the video match officials</b> &mdash; not to the referee. The VAR team then <b>validates the proposal</b>: they manually check the automatically selected kick point and the automatically drawn offside line, built from the players' tracked limbs. Only after that validation does the on-field referee get involved. FIFA's own framing is explicit about why: complex scenarios &mdash; multiple incidents, blocked views, involvement questions &mdash; still need judgement, and involvement is a Laws question no sensor can answer.</p>
<h2>The animation is the receipt</h2>
<p>The broadcast 3D replay is not decoration: it is generated from <b>exactly the same positional data</b> used for the decision, showing the limbs at the kick moment. That is the system's transparency trick &mdash; the viewer sees the evidence the VAR saw. What the animation cannot show is involvement, so the familiar caveat survives the technology: the machine says where the bodies were; the officials say what the play meant.</p>
<h2>What it changed &mdash; and what it did not</h2>
<p>The measurable win is speed: what took VAR teams minutes of manual line-drawing now resolves in seconds, which is why tight offside calls stopped eating five-minute holes in matches. What it did not change is the argument. Millimetre precision makes the marginal calls <em>more</em> exact, not less annoying &mdash; an armpit over the line is now indisputably over the line. The desk's offside explainer covers the law this technology enforces; the goal-line technology explainer covers its binary older sibling. Semi-automated offside is the most sophisticated piece of refereeing assistance football has built, pointed at the one law where precision was always the missing ingredient.</p>""",
[("FIFA — semi-automated offside technology (technical documentation)", "https://inside.fifa.com/innovation/world-cup-2022/semi-automated-offside-technology"),
 ("Wikipedia — semi-automated offside technology", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semi-automated_offside_technology")],
[("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained"),
 ("how-goal-line-technology-works", "How goal-line technology works"),
 ("how-var-works", "How VAR actually works")]),

("how-football-academies-work", "How football academies work &mdash; and how they get paid",
"Training compensation, the solidarity mechanism and homegrown quotas: the money flows that decide whether youth development is an investment or charity.",
"""<p>Every club says it believes in youth. The reason academies survive is that football's rules made youth development <b>pay</b> &mdash; through two global mechanisms, plus league rules that make homegrown players worth roster slots. This is how the pipeline is financed.</p>
<h2>Training compensation: the bill that follows the first contract</h2>
<p>Under FIFA's Regulations on the Status and Transfer of Players, when a player signs his <b>first professional contract</b> &mdash; or moves internationally before the end of the season of his 23rd birthday &mdash; the new club owes <b>training compensation</b> to every club that trained him between the seasons of his <b>12th and 21st birthdays</b>. The amount is not negotiated: it is calculated from standardised training costs published per country and club category, multiplied by the years each club contributed. A small club that coached a boy from 12 to 16 has a claim on his first professional signature, wherever in the world it happens.</p>
<h2>The solidarity mechanism: a slice of every future fee</h2>
<p>The second mechanism never expires. On <b>any international transfer of a contracted player</b>, at any age, <b>5% of the transfer fee</b> is carved out and distributed proportionally to the clubs that trained him between ages 12 and 23. A striker sold at 29 for a large fee still sends slices back to the youth clubs that first registered him &mdash; which is why small clubs watch their graduates' careers like an annuity portfolio, and why the phrase &ldquo;sell-on&rdquo; in transfer reporting often means solidarity rather than a negotiated clause.</p>
<h2>Homegrown rules: the demand side</h2>
<p>Regulation created the supply-side incentives; leagues created demand. UEFA's club competitions require a minimum number of <b>homegrown players</b> in squads (locally trained and association-trained), and the Premier League's squad rules do the same domestically &mdash; non-homegrown places are capped, so academy graduates become the only way to field a full squad legally. Add the accounting asymmetry the desk has covered elsewhere &mdash; graduates carry near-zero book value, so selling them books almost pure profit &mdash; and the commercial logic of a strong academy is complete.</p>
<h2>The honest caveats</h2>
<p>The system works unevenly. Compensation claims are only as good as a small club's paperwork and persistence; intra-country transfers often fall outside FIFA's international mechanisms entirely (domestic rules fill the gap, unevenly); and the economics still tilt towards the clubs who can afford the best coaches, facilities and scouts in the first place. But the design intent holds: a boy's development now leaves a financial trail that follows him for his whole career, and that trail is the reason academies are businesses rather than charity.</p>""",
[("FIFA — Regulations on the Status and Transfer of Players (official PDF)", "https://digitalhub.fifa.com/m/19e76b9a9a9ae0ce/original/adi1292xtnibmwrqnimy-pdf.pdf"),
 ("Mondaq — demystifying fees and financial compensation in football transfers", "https://www.mondaq.com/nigeria/sport/1468300/demystifying-fees-and-financial-compensation-in-football-transfers")],
[("how-transfer-fee-amortisation-works", "How transfer-fee amortisation works"),
 ("why-football-transfers-collapse", "Why transfers collapse"),
 ("how-do-football-clubs-make-money", "How football clubs make money")]),

("how-womens-champions-league-works", "How the Women's Champions League works",
"The 18-team league phase, the knockout ladder, and the fast-professionalising domestic pyramid behind it: the women's club game, explained.",
"""<p>The UEFA Women's Champions League is the apex of the fastest-growing competition structure in football &mdash; and in 2025/26 it adopted the same medicine as the men's game: out with the small groups, in with a single big league phase. The format is worth understanding because the women's club pyramid underneath it is professionalising in real time.</p>
<h2>The current format: one league, eighteen teams</h2>
<p>Eighteen clubs qualify through domestic performance (entry rounds depend on each country's coefficient), and since 2025/26 they contest a <b>single league phase</b>: each team plays <b>six different opponents</b> &mdash; three at home, three away &mdash; drawn from three seeding pots so the schedule balances. All eighteen are ranked in one table; the <b>top four go straight to the quarter-finals</b>, places <b>five to twelve play two-legged play-offs</b> for the other four quarter-final spots, and thirteen to eighteen are out. From the quarter-finals it is the traditional two-legged knockout into a single final at a neutral venue.</p>
<h2>The domestic pyramid feeding it</h2>
<p>Entry comes from the domestic leagues, which are themselves consolidating. England's Women's Super League &mdash; fully professional since its 2018 relaunch &mdash; has been expanding (moving to a 14-club top flight with a second professional division beneath it), and its top clubs qualify for the UWCL, with entry rounds set by England's coefficient. The pattern repeats across Europe: bigger attendances (the game now fills 60,000-seat stadiums at the top end), broadcast deals, and professional contracts replacing the semi-professional era &mdash; which changes what qualification is worth, year on year.</p>
<h2>Why the format change matters</h2>
<p>The move from small groups to a single league phase does what it did in the men's game: more meaningful fixtures, more variety of opposition, and every match feeding one table until the final night, when all games kick off together. For a competition still building its audience, the league phase is also a commercial machine &mdash; big clubs meet big clubs earlier and more often, which is exactly what broadcast schedules want.</p>
<h2>The honest state of play</h2>
<p>The gaps are real and worth stating plainly: revenue remains a fraction of the men's game, squad depth outside the top clubs is thin, and the calendar now collides with an expanding international schedule. But the direction is unambiguous &mdash; professional domestic pyramids, a Champions League format built for television, and attendances that were unimaginable a decade ago. The competition is not waiting for permission to matter; the format change is it arriving.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia — UEFA Women's Champions League", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UEFA_Women%27s_Champions_League"),
 ("Wikipedia — Women's Super League", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women%27s_Super_League")],
[("champions-league-new-format-explained", "The new Champions League format"),
 ("how-the-club-world-cup-works", "How the Club World Cup works")]),
]


# ---- Batch #3 (2026-09-25): scouting, PSR, Euros, relegation economics, medicals ----
SPORT_EXPLAINERS.extend([
    ('how-football-scouting-works', 'How football scouting works: the eye, the data and the decision', 'Live reports, video rooms and data models: how clubs actually find players, what scouts look for, and why the final call is never one number.', '<p>Every transfer starts long before the handshake photo: it starts with someone watching a player nobody else has booked a flight for. Scouting is the least visible department in football and the one that decides whether a &pound;40 million signing was genius or negligence. Here is how the machine actually works.</p><h2>Two scouting jobs, constantly confused</h2><p><b>Player scouting</b> answers <i>who should we sign?</i> &mdash; finding talent in other clubs, academies and lower leagues. <b>Opposition scouting</b> answers <i>how do we beat Saturday\'s opponent?</i> &mdash; analysing the next team\'s patterns, set pieces and weaknesses. Both are called &ldquo;scouting&rdquo;; they share almost no workflow. The rest of this piece is about the first kind.</p><h2>The eye: what a scout is actually watching</h2><p>A live scout watches a player across several matches, in different systems and against different opponents, because one good game against a weak side proves very little. The classic checklist is technique (first touch under pressure, both feet), physical profile (speed, endurance, duels), tactical understanding (positioning off the ball, decision speed) and temperament (reaction to mistakes, to being fouled, to the crowd). Scouts write structured reports, not vibes &mdash; graded against the club\'s own profile for the position, and ideally re-watched on video afterwards to check the live impression survived the replay.</p><h2>The data: what the models add</h2><p>Data does not replace the eye; it shortlists. Clubs screen thousands of players through event data (passes, pressures, carries, duels) and tracking data to find profiles that fit &mdash; a left-back whose progressive carries sit in the league\'s top decile, a striker whose non-penalty xG per 90 survives a change of team. The models are good at <b>finding</b> and bad at <b>finishing</b>: they cannot see a hamstring that nags, a dressing room, or whether a 19-year-old wants to leave home. Every serious club lands on the same division of labour: data narrows the field, live scouting grades the person, video bridges the two.</p><h2>From shortlist to signature</h2><p>A realistic pipeline looks like this: the model flags a profile &rarr; video scouts watch 5&ndash;10 full matches &rarr; live scouts watch in person, home and away &rarr; the recruitment meeting argues profile fit, squad need and price &rarr; background checks (agent, family situation, medical history) &rarr; the club decides whether to open talks. The sporting director owns the process; the manager owns the veto or the blessing. Where clubs fail is usually not the data &mdash; it is skipping steps: a great tournament, a panic deadline, a chairman\'s favourite, and five pipeline stages collapse into one phone call.</p><h2>Why scouting fails anyway</h2><p>Honest failure modes: small samples (20 matches is not a player), system dependence (a pressing forward looked elite in a pressing team), age curve misreads, and the simplest one &mdash; the player is good and the club is wrong for them. The academies that feed this pipeline have the same problem one stage earlier, which is what the <a href="/sports/how-football-academies-work/">academies explainer</a> covers. And when the pipeline\'s final check &mdash; the medical &mdash; changes the answer, the <a href="/sports/how-football-transfer-medicals-work/">medical explainer</a> picks up the story.</p>', [('Wikipedia &mdash; Scout (association football)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scout_(association_football)'), ('Wikipedia &mdash; Transfer window', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfer_window')], [('how-football-academies-work', 'How football academies work'), ('how-football-transfer-medicals-work', 'What happens at a transfer medical'), ('what-does-a-sporting-director-do', 'What a sporting director actually does')]),
    ('how-psr-and-points-deductions-work', 'PSR and points deductions: the rules behind the headlines', "The Premier League's £105m rule, what counts as a loss, the add-backs, and how breaches become deductions &mdash; the mechanics, dated honestly.", '<p>When a club is charged under the Profit and Sustainability Rules (PSR), the headlines say &ldquo;£105 million rule&rdquo; and the arguments start. The mechanics underneath are less dramatic and more interesting &mdash; and they explain why identical losses can produce a deduction for one club and a shrug for another.</p><h2>The rule in one paragraph</h2><p>The Premier League assesses every club\'s <b>Adjusted Earnings Before Tax over a rolling three-year period</b>. Losses up to &pound;15 million across that window need no explanation; losses up to <b>&pound;105 million</b> are permitted if the owners can show the excess is covered by secure funding. Beyond that, the club is in breach. The threshold drops by &pound;22 million for each season spent in the Championship inside the assessment period &mdash; which is why a newly promoted club faces a much tighter ceiling than a club that never left. These are the Premier League\'s own numbers, published in its handbook and statements.</p><h2>What doesn\'t count: the add-backs</h2><p>The &ldquo;Adjusted&rdquo; in Adjusted EBT does heavy lifting. Costs the league deems in the wider interest of the club and the game are added back before the total is struck: investment in <b>academies and youth development, women\'s football, community programmes and infrastructure</b>, plus depreciation of tangible assets. Two clubs with identical headline losses can therefore sit on opposite sides of the line &mdash; one spent on a training ground and a women\'s team, the other on wages. This is also why selling academy graduates looks so powerful on the books: a homegrown player carries little or no amortised book value, so the sale lands as near-pure accounting profit (the same arithmetic the <a href="/sports/how-transfer-fee-amortisation-works/">amortisation explainer</a> and its <a href="/sports/transfer-amortisation-calculator/">calculator</a> show line by line).</p><h2>From breach to deduction</h2><p>A breach is referred to an <b>independent commission</b>, which decides the sanction. Deductions are not automatic or fixed-size: commissions weigh the size and duration of the breach, the club\'s cooperation, whether finances were misstated, and precedent from earlier cases &mdash; which is why Everton\'s and Nottingham Forest\'s 2023&ndash;24 cases produced different numbers for breaches of different shapes. Deductions apply immediately in the current table; appeals go to a separate board. UEFA runs a parallel but distinct system (squad-cost rules targeting 70% of revenue from 2025/26), so a club can be compliant in one and not the other.</p><h2>Why it changed behaviour</h2><p>PSR is why modern windows look the way they do: multi-year payment plans spread the accounting load, player sales are timed before the assessment cut-off, wages are structured with achievable bonuses, and loan deals with obligations quietly become permanent. None of this is loophole-hunting per se &mdash; it is clubs managing to the rule they are given, while the league debates replacing PSR with squad-cost limits modelled on UEFA\'s. Rules of the road: the numbers here were checked against the Premier League\'s published statements as of <b>2026-09-25</b>; the exact handbook wording evolves, and club cases are decided on their own facts.</p>', [('Premier League &mdash; PSR statement (Leicester City referral)', 'https://www.premierleague.com/en/news/3938339'), ('Reuters &mdash; PSR to remain in place next season', 'https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/premier-leagues-profit-sustainability-rules-remain-place-next-season-2025-02-13/'), ('Wikipedia &mdash; Premier League', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Premier_League')], [('financial-fair-play-explained', 'Financial Fair Play, explained'), ('how-transfer-fee-amortisation-works', 'How transfer-fee amortisation works'), ('how-do-football-clubs-make-money', 'How football clubs make money')]),
    ('how-the-euros-work', 'How the Euros work: qualification, groups and the knockout maze', 'From 55 associations to one champion: qualification, the group-stage maths, and the round-of-16 bracket you should read before you watch.', '<p>The European Championship is the densest prize in international football: every participant is a serious nation, there are no minnow group games to coast through, and one bad evening ends a generation\'s best chance. Its format is also the most quietly confusing in the sport. Here is the whole machine.</p><h2>Qualification: the long road</h2><p>All UEFA members (around 55 associations) enter qualification, drawn into groups playing home and away. Group winners and runners-up qualify directly. The remaining places are decided by <b>play-offs</b>, whose entrants come largely from the UEFA Nations League &mdash; the competition created partly to give the international calendar stakes, as the <a href="/sports/how-the-nations-league-works/">Nations League explainer</a> sets out. The host nation (or nations &mdash; 2028 will be co-hosted across the UK and Ireland) qualifies automatically and skips the maze entirely.</p><h2>The group stage: six groups, and the third-place lottery</h2><p>Since 2016 the finals have run with <b>24 teams in six groups of four</b>. The top two in each group advance (12 teams), and &mdash; the part that surprises everyone &mdash; the <b>four best third-placed teams</b> also go through, ranked on points, then goal difference, then goals scored, then fairness and coefficient. The practical effect: four points almost always advances you, three points usually does, and a third-place side can top-scoring its group into the soft side of the bracket or concede late into the hard side. Tiebreakers inside groups run head-to-head first &mdash; the exact order the <a href="/sports/league-tiebreak-calculator/">tiebreak calculator</a> walks through step by step.</p><h2>The knockout maze</h2><p>The round of 16 bracket is fixed in advance: which group winners and third-placed teams feed which ties is published before the tournament, so the entire route to the final is knowable on day one. That creates genuine strategy &mdash; groups finishing in a given order can see, on paper, whether the quarter-final awaits a rival or a resolvable problem. From the round of 16 it is single elimination: 90 minutes, then extra time, then penalties &mdash; the sequence the <a href="/sports/how-extra-time-and-penalty-shootouts-work/">extra-time explainer</a> covers in full. There is no third-place playoff since 1984; the two losing semi-finalists simply go home.</p><h2>Why it stays special</h2><p>The Euros have no weak nights. Club football\'s Champions League gives giants a group stage to stroll through; the Euros do not &mdash; the format\'s density means reigning champions regularly exit in groups, and the trophy has changed hands in almost every direction the game has produced: Catenaccio Italy, Total Football\'s heirs, Spain\'s three-peat era, Portugal on penalties in 2016, Italy on penalties in 2021. A month, seven games maximum, no margin &mdash; which is exactly why every squad announcement, every group draw and every third-place permutation gets argued like a final. Because in this format, they all nearly are.</p>', [('Wikipedia &mdash; UEFA European Championship', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UEFA_European_Championship'), ('BBC Sport &mdash; European Championship', 'https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/european-championship')], [('how-the-nations-league-works', 'How the Nations League works'), ('how-extra-time-and-penalty-shootouts-work', 'Extra time and shootouts, explained'), ('how-the-champions-league-works', 'How the Champions League works')]),
    ('what-happens-when-a-club-is-relegated', 'What happens when a club is relegated: the money, the players, the parachute', 'The revenue cliff, the contract clauses, the parachute payments &mdash; and why the Championship becomes two competitions.', '<p>Relegation is described as a sporting event, but it is really a financial detonation with sporting consequences. Understanding what happens in the months after the final whistle explains half of modern football\'s strangest behaviour &mdash; and why the second tier has become a league within a league.</p><h2>The revenue cliff</h2><p>Premier League clubs earn enormous central payments from broadcasting alone &mdash; the domestic and international deals, facility fees and merit payments together run into nine figures a season. The Championship\'s central payments are a fraction of that. So on the day a club finishes 18th, its income does not fall gradually; it falls off a step, while its costs &mdash; wages above all &mdash; were signed for the world it just left. That mismatch, not sadness, is the actual emergency.</p><h2>The parachute</h2><p>Parachute payments exist to slow the fall. Relegated clubs receive Premier League solidarity payments for up to three seasons &mdash; under the current structure roughly <b>55% of the broadcast revenue they would have earned in year one, 45% in year two and 20% in year three</b>, stopping immediately if they go back up. BBC Sport\'s reporting has the detail, including the total on offer (over &pound;100m for a club relegated after multiple top-flight seasons). The side effect is structural: parachute clubs can outspend the rest of the Championship by design, which is why promoted sides so often come straight back down through the play-offs &mdash; the EFL has called the resulting gap &ldquo;a major concern&rdquo; and independent reports have found parachute recipients are far less likely to be relegated from the Championship. The division is, financially, two competitions running in parallel.</p><h2>The squad: clauses and exits</h2><p>Modern contracts anticipate the drop. Relegation clauses reduce wages automatically (often by 30&ndash;50%), release clauses activate at discounted prices, and loan deals end or convert. The club\'s two asset classes move in opposite directions: the players who attracted interest are sold to balance the books, while the squad must stay good enough to bounce back &mdash; because promotion is worth, by the new broadcast deal, a share of &pound;6.7bn over four seasons. Selling everything to survive guarantees failure next year; selling nothing guarantees a PSR problem &mdash; the <a href="/sports/how-psr-and-points-deductions-work/">PSR explainer</a> shows the ceiling waiting either way.</p><h2>The long shadow</h2><p>The clubs that handle relegation well treat it as a planned rebuild: honour the parachute years with a wage structure built for the Championship, protect three or four saleable assets, promote from the academy (whose graduates book to almost nothing, per the <a href="/sports/how-transfer-fee-amortisation-works/">amortisation explainer</a>). The clubs that handle it badly &mdash; and the history is full of them, from Leeds\' 2004 collapse onward &mdash; keep top-flight wages on Championship income, sell the academy instead of the first team, and turn one bad season into a lost decade. Relegation is survivable. Relegation <i>unplanned</i> is what kills clubs.</p>', [("BBC Sport &mdash; parachute payments gap 'a major concern'", 'https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/65323385'), ('BBC Sport &mdash; how much are the EFL play-off finals worth?', 'https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cp92z8dlv8xo'), ('Wikipedia &mdash; Promotion and relegation', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Promotion_and_relegation')], [('promotion-and-relegation-explained', 'Promotion and relegation, explained'), ('how-psr-and-points-deductions-work', 'PSR and points deductions'), ('how-do-football-clubs-make-money', 'How football clubs make money')]),
    ('how-football-transfer-medicals-work', 'What happens at a transfer medical: the exam that can kill a deal', 'Cardiac screening, MRI scans, movement tests: why there is no pass or fail at a football medical, and what happens when a move collapses.', '<p>The deal is agreed, the shirt is printed, and then the player disappears into a clinic for a day. The transfer medical is football\'s last checkpoint &mdash; and the most misunderstood four hours in the business, because almost everyone describes it as a pass-or-fail exam. It isn\'t.</p><h2>What actually happens in the room</h2><p>A full medical is a head-to-toe audit, and club doctors describe an ideal one taking about four hours: a complete <b>medical history</b> (every operation, every recurring niggle, family cardiac history), a physical examination of the whole body, and investigations &mdash; <b>blood tests, MRI scans of old injury sites, and cardiac screening</b> including an ECG and often an echocardiogram. Cardiac screening is not optional theatre; sudden cardiac events in young athletes are rare and catastrophic, and the screening protocols exist because of the cases that were not caught. How deep the checks go depends on time: a deadline-day signing at 10pm gets the essentials, a planned June signing gets the full day.</p><h2>There is no pass or fail</h2><p>The medic\'s job is a report, not a verdict. Dr Charlotte Cowie, who ran medical departments at Tottenham and Fulham, puts it plainly in BBC interviews: <b>&ldquo;One man\'s fail is another man\'s pass&rdquo;</b> &mdash; the finding is assessed against what the buying club needs, for how long, and at what price. A knee with worn cartilage might end a five-year &pound;40 million deal and barely register for a one-year loan. The club weighs risk against benefit exactly like a house buyer reading a survey: proceed, renegotiate the structure (shorter contract, appearance-weighted wages, buy option instead of obligation), or walk. The medical gives the map; the club chooses the road.</p><h2>When deals die at the medical</h2><p>The famous collapses follow the pattern. Ruud van Nistelrooy\'s 2000 move to Manchester United was delayed by medical concerns &mdash; he ruptured his ACL a week later. Lo&iuml;c Remy\'s &pound;8m Liverpool move in 2014 fell through on medical findings; he signed for Chelsea weeks later, which tells you exactly how club-specific the judgment is. George Boyd\'s 2013 move to Nottingham Forest collapsed over an <b>inconclusive eye test</b> &mdash; and he kept playing elsewhere for a decade. More recently, Kurt Zouma\'s 2024 move to Shabab Al-Ahli and Jean-Philippe Mat&egrave;ta\'s January 2026 deadline-day move to AC Milan both ended at this checkpoint. None of these players were &ldquo;failed&rdquo;; each was a risk one club priced differently than another.</p><h2>The information problem</h2><p>The medical\'s quiet weakness is upstream: selling clubs are not obliged to hand over the full medical file, and histories get presented generously. That is why buying clubs run their own scans rather than trusting paperwork, and why the best recruitment departments keep long-term medical records on targets years before they bid &mdash; scouting the body as well as the player, as the <a href="/sports/how-football-scouting-works/">scouting explainer</a> describes. And when the deal survives the clinic, the fee it just cleared becomes an accounting object with a schedule of its own &mdash; the <a href="/sports/how-transfer-fee-amortisation-works/">amortisation explainer</a> takes it from there.</p>', [('BBC Sport &mdash; what happens during a football medical?', 'https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cj4w287qnn4o'), ('BBC News &mdash; what happens in a football medical?', 'https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-30269991')], [('how-football-scouting-works', 'How football scouting works'), ('why-football-transfers-collapse', 'Why football transfers collapse'), ('how-football-contracts-work', 'How football contracts work')]),
])


SPORT_EXPLAINERS.extend([

("womens-super-league-explained", "The Women's Super League, explained",
"England's top-flight women's league in plain terms: how it is structured, how clubs qualify for Europe, and why the last decade changed everything.",
"""<p>English women's football spent most of its history outside the professional game. The Women's Super League changed that, and it is now the reference model other countries measure themselves against. Here is how it works and why it matters.</p>
<h2>The structure</h2>
<p>The WSL sits at the top of the women's pyramid, with twelve fully professional clubs playing a home-and-away double round-robin &mdash; each team meets every other twice, once at each ground. The champions are crowned on points; the bottom club is relegated to the Women's Championship, the professional second tier, whose winner comes up. Promotion and relegation connects the WSL to the pyramid in a way the franchise leagues of North America do not &mdash; the <a href="/sports/promotion-and-relegation-explained/">promotion guide</a> covers the mechanics. The league turned fully professional in 2018, a line in the sand: clubs had to offer players a minimum number of contracted hours per week, which ended the era of top players holding day jobs.</p>
<h2>Europe and the money</h2>
<p>The top WSL clubs qualify for the UEFA Women's Champions League, the competition that now carries the sport's biggest club revenues &mdash; see the <a href="/sports/how-womens-champions-league-works/">women's Champions League guide</a>. Domestic growth has been just as decisive: broadcast deals moved from niche slots to headline networks, attendances at the big derbies moved from four figures to regular five-figure crowds at major stadiums, and sponsorship money followed the audiences. In 2024 the league entered a new era under a dedicated company owned by the clubs themselves, separating the women's professional leagues from the FA's day-to-day running &mdash; the governance shape men's football has long had.</p>
<h2>Why it matters beyond England</h2>
<p>The WSL's rise was not isolated. It coincided with the <a href="/sports/nwsl-explained/">NWSL</a> professionalising the American game, the Women's World Cup becoming a genuinely global television event, and national associations treating the women's pyramid as core business rather than an outreach programme. The honest read on the league today: salaries and facilities still trail the men's game enormously, squad depth outside the top clubs is thin, and the gap between the leading handful and the rest is the league's defining tension. But the direction is the story &mdash; a competition that began with semi-professional squads playing to hundreds now fills stadiums and sets the tactical and commercial agenda for women's club football worldwide.</p>""",
[("The FA \u2014 Women's Super League", "https://www.thefa.com/womens/wsl"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Women's Super League", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women%27s_Super_League")],
[("how-womens-champions-league-works", "How the Women's Champions League works"), ("nwsl-explained", "The NWSL, explained"), ("womens-world-cup-explained", "The Women's World Cup, explained")]),

("nwsl-explained", "The NWSL, explained: America's women's league runs on different rules",
"The National Women's Soccer League in plain terms: the franchise model, no promotion or relegation, the draft, the salary cap and what makes it different.",
"""<p>The National Women's Soccer League is the top division of women's football in the United States, and it is built on North American sporting logic that European fans often find bewildering. Understanding the differences explains both the league's stability and its quirks.</p>
<h2>Franchises, not clubs</h2>
<p>NWSL teams are franchises in closed competition: there is no promotion or relegation, no pyramid underneath feeding clubs upward. New teams join by expansion &mdash; the league has grown from eight clubs in its 2013 debut season to sixteen, with each addition an ownership group paying an expansion fee and building a stadium plan. The trade-off: owners get territory and security, which has attracted serious investment and purpose-built stadiums; the sport loses the jeopardy that makes European leagues dramatic each spring. It is the same structural fork explained in the <a href="/sports/promotion-and-relegation-explained/">promotion and relegation guide</a>, resolved the American way.</p>
<h2>Building a squad</h2>
<p>Historically the league allocated national-team players to clubs centrally and held an annual college draft &mdash; mechanisms that made NWSL rosters partly decided by administrators rather than managers. Both have been dismantled: the draft was abolished after 2024, and players now move largely as free agents at the end of contracts, as they do everywhere else in the world. A salary cap and roster rules still shape squads, with allocation money allowing clubs to exceed individual maximums &mdash; the North American answer to the Financial Fair Play questions covered in the <a href="/sports/financial-fair-play-explained/">FFP explainer</a>. The result is the most balanced top division in world football: different clubs have won the title and the playoffs year after year, because parity rules work exactly as designed.</p>
<h2>Where it stands</h2>
<p>The league's regular season runs spring to autumn &mdash; the opposite of Europe's calendar &mdash; and ends in playoffs rather than a table-top coronation, with the regular-season leaders winning the Shield and the knockout winners the Championship. Its relationship with US Soccer has loosened as the federation stepped back from centrally funding the league. Attendances and valuations have climbed steeply, and stars from across the world now treat it as a destination league rather than a postscript to European careers. The comparison that frames everything: the <a href="/sports/womens-super-league-explained/">WSL</a> buys drama through jeopardy and history; the NWSL buys it through parity and spectacle. Both models are winning fans, which is the healthiest sign the women's club game has ever had.</p>""",
[("NWSL \u2014 official site", "https://www.nwslsoccer.com/"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 National Women's Soccer League", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Women%27s_Soccer_League")],
[("womens-super-league-explained", "The Women's Super League, explained"), ("womens-world-cup-explained", "The Women's World Cup, explained"), ("promotion-and-relegation-explained", "Promotion and relegation, explained")]),

("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work: leagues, knockouts and the hybrids",
"Round-robin, single elimination, group stage plus knockout, Swiss \u2014 the four architectures behind every competition, and what each one optimises for.",
"""<p>Every competition in sport is an answer to one question: how do you crown a winner fairly when nobody can play everyone forever? There are four basic architectures. Once you see them, any new tournament announces its priorities in its format.</p>
<h2>League: everyone plays everyone</h2>
<p>The round-robin &mdash; a domestic league season &mdash; has every entrant meet every other, usually home and away. It is the fairest test available: the table reflects a season, not a night. Its costs are fixture congestion and a predictable finish, which is why football added jeopardy through the <a href="/sports/promotion-and-relegation-explained/">promotion race and the relegation fight</a>, and why title races that go to the last day are treated as gifts. Round-robins scale badly: sixteen teams home-and-away is 480 matches, which is why cups use everything below.</p>
<h2>Knockout: one night decides</h2>
<p>Single elimination &mdash; the FA Cup, the World Cup from the last thirty-two onward, every playoff system &mdash; doubles the field efficiently and manufactures drama: one bad night ends a favourite. The fairness cost is real (the better team loses plenty), so knockouts are usually seeded to keep the strongest apart until late rounds. Two-legged ties &mdash; a match at each club's ground, decided on aggregate &mdash; were long European football's way of softening that coin-flip quality, before UEFA abolished the away-goals rule and moved back toward one-night drama. When knockout ties cannot be separated, <a href="/sports/how-extra-time-and-penalty-shootouts-work/">extra time and penalties</a> finish them.</p>
<h2>The hybrid and the Swiss</h2>
<p>The group stage plus knockout &mdash; the World Cup template &mdash; splits entrants into small round-robins whose survivors enter a knockout tree. It guarantees every qualifier multiple matches (essential when teams travel continents) while keeping the final stages brutal. The 2026 expansion to forty-eight teams re-engineered the groups, explained in the <a href="/sports/how-the-2026-world-cup-format-works/">World Cup format guide</a>. The newest architecture is the Swiss system: one big table where each team plays a set number of opponents drawn by ranking &mdash; strong meet strong as rounds progress. UEFA adopted it for the Champions League in 2024; the <a href="/sports/champions-league-new-format-explained/">new-format explainer</a> works through the mechanics.</p>
<p>The pattern underneath: round-robins maximise fairness, knockouts maximise drama, hybrids buy guaranteed fixtures, and Swiss systems scale fairness to large fields. Every format is a weighting of those four values &mdash; which is why format changes are never neutral. They decide which kind of champion a competition crowns.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 tournament", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tournament"),
 ("FIFA \u2014 FIFA Women's World Cup", "https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/womens/womensworldcup")],
[("how-the-2026-world-cup-format-works", "How the 2026 World Cup format works"), ("champions-league-new-format-explained", "The new Champions League format, explained"), ("how-extra-time-and-penalty-shootouts-work", "How extra time and penalty shootouts work")]),

("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work: the machinery behind the Games",
"The IOC, host city selection, the sport programme, qualification and universality places \u2014 the structure that turns 200 countries into one fortnight.",
"""<p>The Olympic Games are the largest regularly scheduled event on earth, and their machinery is stranger and more interesting than the broadcast suggests. Four pieces hold it together.</p>
<h2>The IOC and the rings' owners</h2>
<p>The International Olympic Committee owns the Games &mdash; the rings, the broadcast rights, the brand. National Olympic Committees (one per country or territory, over two hundred of them) select and send teams; international federations run each sport and set the qualification standards. The IOC sits above both, and its decisions move sport: it adds and drops sports, sets the host-city agenda, and has run the Summer and Winter Games on staggered four-year cycles since 1994. The commercial engine is equally central: a small number of global sponsors plus broadcast deals fund the movement, which is why host cities increasingly demand the IOC carry more of the bill.</p>
<h2>Choosing hosts and building the programme</h2>
<p>Host cities are elected by IOC members years ahead &mdash; a process reformed after the corruption scandals of the bidding era, now built around using existing venues and long-term plans rather than prestige construction. The sport programme is a rolling negotiation: a core of established sports, with hosts proposing additions that reflect their markets &mdash; which is how skateboarding, surfing and sport climbing entered, and how each host reshapes the slate. Los Angeles 2028, for instance, brings baseball-softball back alongside new additions. The programme decides who gets to be an Olympian as surely as any trial does.</p>
<h2>Qualification: quotas, standards and universality</h2>
<p>Athletes qualify through their sport's own system &mdash; world rankings, qualifying tournaments, or minimum standards &mdash; within quotas that cap each country's entries and keep the field global. Alongside merit runs universality: places guaranteed to smaller NOCs so that nearly every nation on earth can send athletes regardless of qualifying results. It is the philosophical heart of the Games &mdash; universality over pure meritocracy &mdash; and the reason the opening parade features countries with two athletes alongside ones with five hundred. The IOC Refugee Olympic Team, introduced in 2016, extends the same principle to displaced athletes who have no NOC to enter them.</p>
<h2>The Games themselves</h2>
<p>Sixteen days, a few thousand events, one medal table that the IOC itself declines to rank officially &mdash; the ranking conventions are a media invention that nations happily feed. The operational scale (an Olympic village, a temporary city of broadcast infrastructure, security at national scale) is why hosting is now mostly a club of wealthy, existing-infrastructure cities. The Paralympics follow in the same venues weeks later under a binding agreement with the same host &mdash; the <a href="/sports/paralympics-explained/">Paralympics explainer</a> covers that side of the fortnight. What the whole machine optimises for, above fairness or profit, is the thing no other event replicates: nearly every sport's best, from nearly every country, in one place, once every four years.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games"),
 ("LA28 \u2014 Los Angeles 2028 organising committee", "https://la28.org/")],
[("paralympics-explained", "The Paralympics, explained"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("womens-world-cup-explained", "The Women's World Cup, explained: from experiment to the biggest stage",
"FIFA's women's flagship in plain terms: the format, the confederation qualification routes, and the tournament's thirty-year climb into the mainstream.",
"""<p>The FIFA Women's World Cup began in 1991 as something close to an experiment &mdash; twelve teams in China, officially billed as a &ldquo;world championship for women's football&rdquo; because even its own organisers hedged the word World Cup. Three decades on it is one of the most-watched events in sport. The mechanics and the story are inseparable.</p>
<h2>The format</h2>
<p>The modern tournament runs on the classic hybrid explained in the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">tournament formats guide</a>: thirty-two teams (expanded from twenty-four in 2023, with forty-eight planned for 2027 in Brazil) drawn into eight groups of four, each playing a round-robin; the top two from each group advance to a straight knockout tree from the round of sixteen to the final. Qualification is by confederation &mdash; UEFA, CONMEBOL, CAF, AFC, CONCACAF and OFC each receive slots weighted toward the strongest regions, with inter-confederation playoffs deciding the last places. It mirrors the men's template deliberately: the comparison with the <a href="/sports/how-the-2026-world-cup-format-works/">men's 2026 format</a> shows how the women's tournament is following the same expansion arc, a decade behind and accelerating.</p>
<h2>The climb</h2>
<p>The growth curve is the story of the competition. Early editions played to thin crowds and minimal coverage; the United States, dominant from the start, won the first two titles and built the sport's first global stars. The turning points stacked: record television audiences through the 2010s, the 2019 edition in France selling out stadiums, and the 2023 co-hosted tournament in Australia and New Zealand breaking attendance and broadcast records across both hemispheres &mdash; with Spain's win, on the back of a club system and a generation developed in Europe's professionalised leagues, announcing that American dominance had an era. Each cycle now feeds the professional club game &mdash; the <a href="/sports/womens-super-league-explained/">WSL</a> and the <a href="/sports/nwsl-explained/">NWSL</a> both grew on the back of it &mdash; and each cycle lifts the competitive floor: the gap between the traditional powers and the rest narrows faster than in the men's game ever did.</p>
<h2>What to watch next</h2>
<p>Brazil 2027 takes the tournament to South America for the first time and expands the field to forty-eight &mdash; more confederation slots, more first-time qualifiers, more group-stage mathematics. The honest open questions: whether expansion dilutes quality before it deepens it, how commercial rights keep pace with the men's tournament's economics, and whether the next champion comes from a federation that was quarter-final fodder a decade ago. On current evidence, bet on the last one. Tournaments like this do not grow linearly &mdash; they grow in jumps, and every jump so far has produced a champion the previous cycle would not have predicted.</p>""",
[("FIFA \u2014 FIFA Women's World Cup", "https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/womens/womensworldcup"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 FIFA Women's World Cup", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_Women%27s_World_Cup")],
[("womens-super-league-explained", "The Women's Super League, explained"), ("nwsl-explained", "The NWSL, explained"), ("how-the-2026-world-cup-format-works", "How the 2026 World Cup format works")]),

("paralympics-explained", "The Paralympics, explained: classification, programme and the fortnight that follows",
"The Paralympic Games in plain terms: the IPC, the classification system that makes competition fair, the sport programme, and the growth story.",
"""<p>The Paralympic Games are the second-largest multi-sport event in the world, and the least understood &mdash; largely because their central innovation, classification, has no equivalent in mainstream sport. Get classification and the whole Games makes sense.</p>
<h2>The structure</h2>
<p>The International Paralympic Committee runs the Games under an agreement that binds them to the Olympics: since the late 1980s, the Paralympics have been held in the same host city, in the same venues, weeks after the closing ceremony &mdash; one organising committee, one infrastructure, two Games. The model is described in the <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> because it is genuinely one machine. Athletes qualify through their sport's own rankings and qualifying events, entered by National Paralympic Committees, with universality places ensuring broad global participation.</p>
<h2>Classification: the idea that makes it fair</h2>
<p>Paralympic sport spans a huge range of impairments &mdash; limb deficiency, cerebral palsy, visual impairment, spinal cord injury and more. Competition would be meaningless if a swimmer with one below-knee amputation raced a swimmer with full spinal cord involvement. So every sport classifies athletes into categories by how much their impairment affects <em>that sport's</em> specific demands &mdash; a visual-impairment class in athletics differs from one in swimming, because the sports differ. Classifiers &mdash; medical and technical experts &mdash; assess athletes against published criteria, and classes are prefixed by the sport's letter (S for swimming, T for track). The system's honest difficulty: it is part medicine, part judgement, it evolves with research, and edge cases &mdash; and the rare bad-faith actor &mdash; generate genuine controversy. But the principle is unarguable: compete like against like, defined by functional effect, not diagnosis.</p>
<h2>The programme and the growth</h2>
<p>The Paralympic programme now runs twenty-plus summer sports, with athletics and swimming the giants by event count, and wheelchair rugby, sitting volleyball and goalball among the sports built specifically for the movement. Winter editions run their own slate. The growth story of the last two decades is the story of the Games becoming an event people watch rather than a gesture hosts make: sold-out venues became normal from London 2012 onward, broadcast hours multiplied, and elite Paralympians became commercial names. The gaps that remain are equally real &mdash; prize money and sponsorship still trail the Olympic equivalents in most countries, and classification disputes occasionally eclipse competition. Neither cancels the central fact: for two weeks every four years, the classification system turns the widest possible range of human bodies into sport judged on the same terms as any other &mdash; training, talent and nerve.</p>""",
[("International Paralympic Committee \u2014 the Paralympic Games", "https://www.paralympic.org/paralympic-games"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Paralympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paralympic_Games")],
[("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

])


SPORT_EXPLAINERS.extend([

("cricket-formats-explained", "Cricket's three formats, explained: Test, one-day and T20",
"The same sport, three clocks: how Test, ODI and T20 cricket differ in rules, tactics and temperament \u2014 and which one to start with.",
"""<p>Cricket confuses newcomers for one reason: it is three sports sharing a rulebook. Test cricket, one-day internationals and Twenty20 use the same pitch, ball and stumps &mdash; and almost nothing else. The format decides everything: tactics, fitness, careers, even which players become stars.</p>
<h2>The three clocks</h2>
<p><b>Test cricket</b> is the marathon: up to five days, two innings per side, no over limit, and draws are a legitimate result &mdash; a team can dominate for four days and lose on the fifth. It is the format that tests technique, concentration and temperament, which is why its records carry the most weight. <b>One-day internationals</b> compress everything into 50 overs per side and roughly eight hours; the World Cup is played in this format, and its defining innovation was the <em>powerplay</em> &mdash; fielding restrictions in set periods that force the scoring rhythm into phases. <b>Twenty20</b> is the sprint: 20 overs, three hours, entertainment-first rules (free hits, strategic timeouts) and a batting style built on risk. The franchise T20 leagues &mdash; the IPL chief among them &mdash; are now the sport's commercial engine; the <a href="/sports/how-the-ipl-works/">IPL explainer</a> covers that machine.</p>
<h2>Why the tactics barely transfer</h2>
<p>A Test batter defends good balls for hours to earn the right to attack; a T20 batter attacks from ball one because 120 balls is the whole innings. Bowlers change accordingly: Tests reward patience and reverse swing with an old ball; T20 rewards variation &mdash; slower balls, yorkers, mystery spin &mdash; because being hit for six twice in an over ends a spell. This is why the three formats increasingly select different specialists, and why "best in the world" now needs a format attached. The World Test Championship, a two-year league deciding a final, was introduced to give the longest format a context modern audiences can follow &mdash; the hybrid structure the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">tournament formats guide</a> describes.</p>
<h2>Which one to start with</h2>
<p>Start with <b>T20</b>: three hours, constant action, no prior knowledge assumed &mdash; it teaches the basic grammar (overs, wickets, run rates) painlessly. Then try a <b>day of a Test</b>, preferably day one or a fourth-day chase, when the sport's chess match is legible. One-day cricket sits between them and hosts the World Cup every four years. The honest summary: T20 is the gateway, ODIs are the compromise, and Tests are the art form &mdash; most fans arrive through the first and stay for the third.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 forms of cricket", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forms_of_cricket"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Twenty20", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twenty20")],
[("how-the-ipl-works", "How the IPL works"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("tennis-scoring-explained", "Tennis scoring, explained: why 15, 30, 40 and deuce",
"Love, 15, 30, 40, deuce, advantage: the strangest scoring system in sport decoded, plus sets, tie-breaks and why matches never end on time.",
"""<p>Fifteen, thirty, forty, love, deuce, ad-in, ad-out: tennis scoring sounds like a card game read aloud by a butler. It is actually a small, coherent machine &mdash; once decoded, it explains why tennis produces more drama per point than almost any sport.</p>
<h2>Points, games, sets: the three-layer clock</h2>
<p>A <b>game</b> is won by the first player to four points with a two-point margin &mdash; the points are called love (zero), 15, 30 and 40 (the folk etymology &mdash; a clock face quartered, "quarante-cinq" corrupted &mdash; is unproven but harmless). At 40&ndash;40, <b>deuce</b>, the two-point margin rule takes over: win one and you hold <b>advantage</b>; win the next and the game is yours; lose it and it is deuce again. Six games with a two-game margin wins a <b>set</b>; at 6&ndash;6 a <b>tie-break</b> usually decides it &mdash; first to seven points, same margin rule. Best of three sets wins most matches; the Grand Slams play men best of five, which is why a Slam match can run five hours while a tour final finishes in ninety minutes.</p>
<h2>Why the system makes drama</h2>
<p>The two-point margin rules &mdash; deuce, and the 6&ndash;6 tie-break &mdash; exist to stop games and sets dragging forever, but they create something no other scoring system does: <b>every single point can be decisive</b>. Break point, set point, match point &mdash; a player 40&ndash;0 down in the final game of the final set is one point from disaster, and the scoreboard says so out loud. Because service alternates every game, matches have no clock: a match ends when the tennis ends, which is why schedules run late and why tennis produces comebacks that timed sports structurally prevent.</p>
<h2>The quirks, briefly</h2>
<p>Serve alternates each game; ends change on odd games; the deciding set at Slams now uses a ten-point tie-break at 6&ndash;6 (recently standardised after decades of marathons, including the 11&ndash;9 fifth set at Wimbledon 2010 that ran over eleven hours across three days). Doubles often replaces the deciding point with a "golden point" and the third set with a match tie-break. And the chair umpire's vocabulary &mdash; "advantage, server" &mdash; is simply the margin rule narrated.</p>
<p>The one-line decoder: tennis is a game of games of sets, and every layer insists on winning by two &mdash; which is exactly why a match can hinge on a single shot, and why the scoring, for all its medieval flavour, has never been seriously challenged.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 tennis scoring system", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennis_scoring_system"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 tennis", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennis")],
[("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work"), ("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work")]),

("rugby-union-vs-league-explained", "Rugby union versus rugby league, explained",
"One origin, two sports: the split that made union and league, and how the rules, tactics and cultures diverged \u2014 plus sevens.",
"""<p>Rugby union and rugby league share a name, a shape of ball and a founding myth, and then diverge so thoroughly that fans of one often cannot follow the other. The split is 130 years old, the differences are structural &mdash; and understanding the split explains everything else.</p>
<h2>The 1895 split, in one paragraph</h2>
<p>Northern English clubs, whose working-class players lost wages to play Saturday matches, asked the Rugby Football Union for compensation. The RFU refused, insisting on strict amateurism; the northern clubs walked out and formed their own union &mdash; which within a decade had reshaped the rules to reward running rugby and punish the stalemates of the old game. "League" became professional decades before union (which turned professional only in 1995), and the two codes evolved separately ever since: union strong across the UK, Ireland, France, the southern hemisphere and beyond; league concentrated in northern England, Queensland and New South Wales, and the Pacific.</p>
<h2>The rules that actually differ</h2>
<p>Union: <b>fifteen players</b>, contested scrums that are genuinely fought, lineouts with lifting, rucks and mauls &mdash; possession is competed for after every tackle, so the game is a sequence of collisions over the ball. Kicking for territory and goal-kicks at penalty are core strategy; tries are worth five points. League: <b>thirteen players</b>, no lineouts, uncontested scrums, and the defining rule &mdash; the tackled team keeps the ball for a <b>set of six tackles</b>, after which it is handed over (usually kicked). Possession is therefore structured like drives in American football, which makes league faster, more continuous and more try-focused (four points). Union's breakdown battles and league's six-tackle chess are the same sport's answer to opposite questions: who deserves the ball, and what do you do with a guaranteed one.</p>
<h2>And then there is sevens</h2>
<p><b>Rugby sevens</b> is union's condensation: seven players, seven-minute halves, the same pitch &mdash; which means open space, sprinting wingers and absurd scores. It is Olympic (the <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> covers how sports enter) and runs a world series built on tournaments rather than leagues &mdash; the knockout-heavy format the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats guide</a> describes. For a newcomer: watch sevens first for the speed, then a league match for the flow, then a union Test for the full tactical war. All three will feel like cousins who grew up in different countries &mdash; because that is precisely what happened.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 rugby football", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rugby_football"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 comparison of rugby league and rugby union", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_rugby_league_and_rugby_union")],
[("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("athletics-track-and-field-explained", "Athletics explained: track, field and the meeting circuit",
"The world's oldest sport in plain terms: event families, how the Diamond League and world rankings work, and what makes records complicated.",
"""<p>Athletics is the sport beneath all sports &mdash; run, jump, throw &mdash; and the simplest to understand badly. The event list looks chaotic; it is actually a small taxonomy, sitting on top of a modern professional circuit most casual viewers never see explained.</p>
<h2>The event families</h2>
<p>Track events split by physiology: <b>sprints</b> (100m to 400m, pure anaerobic power, decided in hundredths), <b>middle distance</b> (800&ndash;1500m, the most tactically brutal races in sport &mdash; too fast to coast, too slow to sprint), <b>distance</b> (5000m, 10,000m and the marathon, where pace management is the whole contest), plus <b>hurdles and relays</b>. Field events split into <b>jumps</b> (long, triple, high jump, pole vault) and <b>throws</b> (shot, discus, javelin, hammer). The combined events &mdash; decathlon for men, heptathlon for women &mdash; compress ten or seven disciplines into two days and are conventionally treated as crowning the most complete athlete.</p>
<h2>The professional circuit, explained</h2>
<p>Outside the Olympics and World Championships, elite athletics runs on <b>meetings</b>: one-night events where athletes are paid appearance fees and prize money, headlined by the <b>Diamond League</b> &mdash; a season-long series whose top performers qualify for a final. Since 2018 the sport also runs a <b>world rankings</b> system that scores performances by result and competition grade, which doubles as the qualification mechanism for global championships &mdash; the same rankings-and-quotas logic the <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> describes. Road running &mdash; the marathon majors and mass participation &mdash; sits parallel to all of it, with the six World Marathon Majors functioning as the sport's de facto grand tours.</p>
<h2>Why records come with fine print</h2>
<p>Athletics records are the most qualified in sport. Wind readings void sprint records above a tailwind limit; altitude marks are flagged because thin air flatters sprints and handicaps distance; the recent shoe-technology era forced World Athletics to legislate sole thickness and even introduced "track records" separate from world marks to keep eras comparable; and every record sits under the anti-doping regime whose history &mdash; entire event marks standing since the 1980s &mdash; is why commentators discuss some records with a raised eyebrow. The honest viewer's guide: enjoy the race, trust the photo-finish, and treat the record book as a document with footnotes &mdash; which, unlike most sports' history books, it literally is.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Diamond League", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_League"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 athletics (sport)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athletics_(sport)")],
[("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work"), ("paralympics-explained", "The Paralympics, explained")]),

("how-f1-weekends-work", "How an F1 weekend works: practice, qualifying, sprints and the race",
"Formula 1's three-day format decoded: sessions, parc ferm\u00e9, the sprint experiment, DRS and why strategy decides more than speed.",
"""<p>An F1 broadcast drops you into the middle of a weekend with a structure it never explains: why are they driving on Friday, what is parc ferm&eacute;, and why did the fastest car finish fourth? The format is the answer &mdash; and it turns twenty drivers into one winner through attrition as much as speed.</p>
<h2>The three days</h2>
<p>Friday and Saturday morning run <b>practice sessions</b> &mdash; setup work, tyre evaluation and (for young drivers) mandatory running. Then <b>qualifying</b>: a three-round knockout (Q1, Q2, Q3) where the slowest are eliminated in each round and the survivors fight for pole &mdash; the purest speed test in the weekend, run on empty tanks and soft tyres. Sunday is the <b>race</b>: 300-ish kilometres, mandatory pit stops because the tyres cannot survive the distance, and the whole grid starting in qualifying order. Selected weekends add a <b>sprint</b> &mdash; a short Saturday race with its own shootout qualifying &mdash; designed as extra spectacle; it remains the sport's most debated experiment, loved by broadcasters and tolerated by purists. All weekend sessions run under <b>parc ferm&eacute;</b> rules: from qualifying onward the cars are effectively locked, so setup gambles must be placed before anyone knows race conditions.</p>
<h2>Why the fastest car does not always win</h2>
<p>Three structural levers make strategy decisive. <b>Tyres</b>: three compounds, each trading pace against durability, and the rules about which sets can start the race mean the pit-stop sequence is a game of chicken &mdash; undercut (stop earlier, exploit fresh-tyre pace) against overcut. <b>Fuel</b>: cars start heavy and get seconds-per-lap faster as they burn, so timing matters more than raw speed. And <b>DRS</b> &mdash; the rear-wing flap that opens in designated zones for cars within a second of the car ahead &mdash; which artificially creates overtaking at the cost of purists' complaints. Add safety cars that compress the field at random and you have the sport's central truth: a Grand Prix is a two-hour resource-management problem wrapped around a speed contest, and the pit wall wins as often as the cockpit.</p>
<h2>The championship arithmetic</h2>
<p>Points descend from first to tenth; drivers and their teams (constructors) score in parallel championships, which is why a team can celebrate third place &mdash; prize money follows the constructors' table. With 24-ish races across five continents, the season rewards consistency: the sprint races and fastest-lap point are marginal next to simply finishing. It is a league across a knockout weekend &mdash; the hybrid logic the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">tournament formats guide</a> sets out &mdash; and it is why F1 fans follow Friday practice: in a sport decided by hundredths and tyre windows, every session is evidence.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Formula One", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Formula_One"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Formula One regulations", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Formula_One_regulations")],
[("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work"), ("how-the-euros-work", "How the Euros work")]),

("how-the-ipl-works", "How the IPL works: franchise cricket's money machine",
"The Indian Premier League in plain terms: the auction, the salary cap, the playoff format \u2014 and how a six-week tournament became cricket's economic centre of gravity.",
"""<p>The Indian Premier League lasts about ten weeks a year and now moves more money than any competition in cricket &mdash; more, in broadcast terms, than everything else in the sport combined. It is also the clearest example in world sport of the franchise model applied to a traditional game. The mechanics explain the power.</p>
<h2>Franchises, auction, salary cap</h2>
<p>Ten city-based franchises &mdash; owned by everyone from Bollywood stars to sovereign-scale investment groups &mdash; compete in a closed league with no promotion or relegation: the same structural choice the <a href="/sports/nwsl-explained/">NWSL explainer</a> covers, with cricket's transfer market on top. Players are not bought freely between seasons; they are acquired through an <b>auction</b>, where franchises bid against each other from purses bounded by a <b>salary cap</b> with squad-composition rules (overseas-player limits, retention slots). The auction is itself a televised spectacle and a genuine skill contest &mdash; squads are built by valuation under pressure, which is why "auction genius" is a real job description.</p>
<h2>The tournament</h2>
<p>The season runs a double round-robin league phase &mdash; every franchise plays the others home and away, each match a T20 of roughly three and a half hours &mdash; then a four-team playoff with a distinctive fairness twist: the <b>qualifier structure</b> gives the top two sides two routes to the final, so the table-toppers are rewarded with a second chance while third and fourth play an elimination knockout. It is the group-plus-knockout hybrid with a double-elimination top, a format choice designed to make every league position matter &mdash; the logic the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">tournament formats guide</a> unpacks.</p>
<h2>Why it moved the centre of cricket</h2>
<p>The IPL's economics reordered the sport's power structure in one generation. Its broadcast deals are among the largest in world sport per match; its salary offers exceed what most national boards can pay per month, so the world's best players organise their calendars around it; and its success spawned a dozen copycat leagues &mdash; a franchise T20 circuit now runs most of the year somewhere on earth. The tension this creates is real and unresolved: club cricket increasingly outpays country cricket, and the calendar is a constant negotiation between leagues, international boards and players' bodies. What is not in doubt: for understanding where cricket is heading &mdash; money, format and audience &mdash; the IPL is the case study, and its six-week season is the sport's most consequential ten weeks.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Indian Premier League", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Premier_League"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 forms of cricket", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forms_of_cricket")],
[("cricket-formats-explained", "Cricket's three formats, explained"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

])


SPORT_EXPLAINERS.extend([

("basketball-rules-explained", "Basketball rules, explained: the shot clock that changed sport",
"Scoring, fouls, violations and the 24-second clock: the rules of basketball decoded, and why its pacing makes it the most clock-conscious game there is.",
"""<p>Basketball looks simple &mdash; put the ball in the hoop &mdash; and its rules are genuinely fewer than football's. But the sport hides one revolutionary invention and one elegant system of consequences, and understanding both explains why it plays the way it does.</p>
<h2>Scoring and the shape of play</h2>
<p>Five players per side, a field goal inside the arc is worth <b>two</b>, beyond it <b>three</b>, a free throw <b>one</b>. The three-pointer &mdash; adopted by the NBA in 1979, dismissed for years as a gimmick &mdash; is now the engine of modern tactics: analytics showed a 33% three-point shooter scores as efficiently as a 50% two-point shooter, and the geometry of the game reorganised around that arithmetic. Possession changes on scores, turnovers and defensive rebounds, and games run in quarters (NBA: four of twelve minutes) with overtime periods until settled &mdash; basketball has no draws.</p>
<h2>The shot clock: sport's most copied idea</h2>
<p>Before 1954, leading teams simply held the ball &mdash; games ended 19&ndash;18. The <b>24-second shot clock</b> forced an attempt within the window or surrendered possession, and it did for basketball what no other rule change has done for any sport: it made stalling structurally impossible. Every possession now has a deadline, which is why basketball produces more scoring events per minute than any major team sport and why its end-of-game mathematics &mdash; fouling to stop the clock, trading twos for threes &mdash; is legible to anyone who knows the clock situation. The <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats guide</a> covers competition structures; the shot clock is the possession-level equivalent &mdash; a rule that decides the game's entire tempo.</p>
<h2>Fouls, violations and the bonus</h2>
<p>Two families of infractions with different logics. <b>Violations</b> (travelling, double dribble, three seconds in the key, shot-clock expiry) simply hand over the ball. <b>Fouls</b> &mdash; illegal contact &mdash; carry accumulation: players foul out at a limit (six in the NBA, five under international rules), and teams that exceed a per-quarter team-foul count enter the <b>bonus</b>, where every subsequent foul gifts free throws. The bonus is the sport's quiet strategic layer: late in quarters, defenders must play cleaner than early, and fouling to stop the clock becomes a priced decision rather than a free one. Technical and flagrant fouls police conduct separately.</p>
<p>The honest beginner's summary: basketball is a game of deadlines &mdash; a shot clock on every possession, a game clock on every quarter, foul limits on every player. Once you watch the clocks instead of just the ball, the sport's decisions stop looking chaotic and start looking like arithmetic played at a sprint.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 basketball rules", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basketball_rules"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 shot clock", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shot_clock")],
[("how-the-nba-works", "How the NBA works"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("how-the-nba-works", "How the NBA works: the league built for parity and drama",
"The NBA's season, the draft and its lottery, the salary cap and the playoff gauntlet \u2014 how the league's machinery turns 30 franchises into one champion.",
"""<p>The NBA is the most global of the American leagues and the most transparent about its own machinery &mdash; a closed franchise system engineered, like the <a href="/sports/nwsl-explained/">NWSL</a>, for competitive balance, but with a superstar economy that makes its parity rules a constant negotiation.</p>
<h2>The season and the gauntlet</h2>
<p>Thirty franchises, no promotion or relegation, an 82-game regular season from October to April &mdash; a marathon designed less to crown the best team than to seed the real competition. Sixteen teams enter the <b>playoffs</b>, every round a best-of-seven series, no draws, two months of elimination basketball ending in the Finals. The long season with a knockout ending is the group-plus-knockout hybrid the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats guide</a> describes, stretched across eight months &mdash; and the best-of-seven structure is deliberately anti-upset: over seven games the better team usually proves it, which is why NBA champions are more predictable than cup-trophy football's.</p>
<h2>The draft lottery: losing as an asset</h2>
<p>The NBA's most distinctive institution is how it distributes new talent: the <b>draft</b>, where the worst teams pick first &mdash; with the exact order for the non-playoff teams settled by a weighted lottery rather than pure reverse standings, a design choice made after tanking scandals proved that handing the top pick to the worst record incentivises losing on purpose. The lottery does not eliminate tanking &mdash; it prices it &mdash; and the tension between rebuilding honestly and rebuilding deliberately is the league's permanent governance argument. Rookie contracts are slotted by draft position, so the value of moving up a few slots is enormous and openly traded.</p>
<h2>The salary cap and the superstar economy</h2>
<p>A <b>soft salary cap</b> bounds spending but leaks through exceptions &mdash; mechanisms that let teams exceed the cap to re-sign their own players, absorb trades or sign minimum contracts. The result is a league where superstars earn maximum salaries while role players are shopped for value, and where stars increasingly choose destinations, making free agency a July spectacle of its own. Luxury taxes punish the biggest spenders without forbidding them &mdash; a different philosophy from European football's <a href="/sports/financial-fair-play-explained/">FFP regime</a>, aiming at parity through pricing rather than prohibition.</p>
<p>The synthesis: the NBA runs a closed league (security for owners), a reverse-order draft (hope for the worst teams), a soft cap (limits with escape hatches) and a best-of-seven playoff (the deserving usually win). It is the most deliberately engineered balance of parity and stardom in world sport &mdash; and its rules change every collective bargaining agreement because the balance never quite settles. That is the league working as designed: the argument is the product.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 National Basketball Association", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Basketball_Association"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 NBA draft", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBA_draft")],
[("basketball-rules-explained", "Basketball rules, explained"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("american-football-rules-explained", "American football rules, explained: the chess clock of the downs",
"Four downs, ten yards, the scoring ladder and the clock games: the rules of American football decoded for people who find it baffling.",
"""<p>American football confuses newcomers for the same reason cricket does &mdash; a stop-start rhythm that looks like chaos until the central mechanic clicks. For football the mechanic is the <b>downs system</b>, and once it lands, the sport reveals itself as turn-based strategy with collisions.</p>
<h2>The core loop: four downs, ten yards</h2>
<p>The offence has <b>four attempts (downs) to advance ten yards</b>. Succeed and the count resets &mdash; a "first down"; fail and the other team takes over where play ended. Everything flows from this: most fourth downs are surrendered voluntarily by <b>punting</b> (kicking the ball away to pin the opponent deep, trading territory for field position), and the decision to "go for it" instead is the sport's recurring gamble. The ten-yard chain, the markers on the sideline, the "1st and 10" graphics &mdash; all of it is bookkeeping for this one rule. Each down ends when the ball-carrier is tackled, the pass falls incomplete, or the ball leaves play, which is why the game is a sequence of discrete, coachable moments rather than a continuous flow.</p>
<h2>Scoring, in descending order of worth</h2>
<p>A <b>touchdown</b> (carrying or catching in the end zone) is six points, followed by a conversion kick for one or a run/pass for two. A <b>field goal</b> &mdash; kicked through the uprights, usually on fourth down when a touchdown looks unlikely &mdash; is three. A <b>safety</b> (tackling the offence in its own end zone) is two, and rare. The scoring ladder creates the sport's signature arithmetic: leading by three, a field goal ties but a touchdown wins, so late-game decisions &mdash; kick or go &mdash; are pure expected-value problems played out live. Games run in four 15-minute quarters, but the clock stops constantly (incompletions, out of bounds, timeouts), so a game consumes three-plus hours &mdash; the stoppages are where the strategy lives.</p>
<h2>The parts newcomers skip</h2>
<p>Each team fields <b>separate offence, defence and special-teams units</b> &mdash; a 53-man roster means players do one job, which is why substitutions are free and constant. Penalties (holding, offsides, pass interference) move the ball five to fifteen yards and, crucially, can gift automatic first downs &mdash; a penalty is not a fine, it is territory. And the quarterback is the sport's decision node: reading the defence before the snap, audibling the play, then passing, handing off or running within seconds.</p>
<p>The honest viewing guide: watch the down and distance ("2nd and 7" means second attempt, seven yards to go), and every decision announces itself. Fourth-down gambles, two-minute clock management, kicking decisions in field-goal range &mdash; American football is the most explicitly mathematical of the major sports, broadcasting its own arithmetic on screen. Learn the loop and it stops being noise.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 American football rules", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_football_rules"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 down (gridiron football)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Down_(gridiron_football)")],
[("how-the-super-bowl-works", "How the Super Bowl works"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("how-the-super-bowl-works", "How the Super Bowl works: the largest single-night event in American sport",
"The playoff road, the single-game final, the halftime machine and why one Sunday carries a city's economics \u2014 the Super Bowl explained.",
"""<p>The Super Bowl is a championship game, a national holiday and a broadcast event fused into one Sunday &mdash; consistently the most-watched annual television broadcast in the United States, with an economics that dwarfs the sport being played. The structure explains the spectacle.</p>
<h2>The road there</h2>
<p>The NFL's 32 franchises split into two conferences &mdash; AFC and NFC &mdash; each running its own season and playoffs in parallel. Fourteen teams qualify for the postseason (seven per conference): the four division winners plus three wildcards, seeded so the best teams host and the top seed rests in the first round. From there it is single-elimination &mdash; the pure knockout the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats guide</a> describes &mdash; through wildcard weekend, divisional rounds and conference championships, until one AFC and one NFC champion remain. One game decides the title: no series, no aggregate, no second chance. The <a href="/sports/american-football-rules-explained/">rules explainer</a> covers the game itself; the Super Bowl is that game at maximum stakes.</p>
<h2>The single-game final and its gravity</h2>
<p>Because one night decides everything, the Super Bowl compresses a season's value into three hours &mdash; the highest-variance championship in American sport and the reason upsets become decades-long civic memories. The NFL leans into the event deliberately: a neutral host city chosen years ahead (bidding for the game is bidding for an estimated hundreds-of-millions economic influx), a two-week media carnival, and scheduling fixed to the first Sunday of a February fortnight &mdash; the calendar itself is a brand.</p>
<h2>The show around the game</h2>
<p>The halftime performance &mdash; twelve minutes that routinely outdraw the Grammy Awards &mdash; is booked as a global music event, and the advertising is a genre of its own: thirty seconds of broadcast reaching over a hundred million viewers commands prices beyond any other inventory on television, which is why the commercials are reviewed like films. The food consumption statistics are recited annually because they are genuinely absurd. None of it is accidental: the NFL engineered the Super Bowl as <b>appointment television</b> before the phrase existed, in an era when live sport is the last programming that still commands a mass simultaneous audience &mdash; the same scarcity logic cinemas meet on an opening weekend, transplanted to one Sunday a year.</p>
<p>The honest summary: the Super Bowl works because the sport's single-elimination final gives one night genuine consequence, and the league wraps that consequence in the biggest entertainment machine in broadcasting. The game decides a champion; everything around it decides how a hundred million people spend a Sunday. Very few events in any industry do both at once.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Super Bowl", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Bowl"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 NFL playoffs", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_playoffs")],
[("american-football-rules-explained", "American football rules, explained"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("golf-scoring-explained", "Golf scoring, explained: par, birdies and the handicap that levels everyone",
"Why golf counts strokes against par, the birds and the bogeys, how handicaps make a weekend player competitive \u2014 and why the format makes every shot matter.",
"""<p>Golf is the only major sport scored against a benchmark rather than an opponent &mdash; you play the course, and the opponent is optional. That inversion explains its scoring vocabulary, its handicap system and why it is the most statistically legible sport there is.</p>
<h2>Par and its birds</h2>
<p>Every hole carries a <b>par</b> &mdash; the number of strokes an expert is expected to need (3, 4 or 5, set mostly by length). Scoring is named relative to it: one under is a <b>birdie</b>, two under an <b>eagle</b>, three under the near-mythical <b>albatross</b>; one over a <b>bogey</b>, two over a double bogey. A round totals eighteen holes, typically par 70&ndash;72, and tournaments aggregate rounds over four days &mdash; scores read as "+2" or "&minus;11" mean strokes relative to the course's total par. The vocabulary sounds quaint; the system is precise: because every hole has its own benchmark, performance is measurable shot by shot, which is why golf produces richer statistics &mdash; strokes gained, proximity to the hole &mdash; than any other sport.</p>
<h2>The handicap: sport's great leveller</h2>
<p>Golf's structural genius is the <b>handicap</b>: a number representing strokes a player receives against the benchmark, calculated from recent scores under a unified world system. A 20-handicap playing a scratch golfer receives twenty strokes distributed by hole difficulty &mdash; and the two can compete legitimately. No other major sport lets a weekend amateur and a professional share a fair contest; the handicap is why golf is played socially for life while other sports retire their players at 35. It also runs on an honour system at its edges &mdash; handicap manipulation ("sandbagging") is the sport's characteristic cheat, policed by committees and algorithms alike.</p>
<h2>Why the format makes every shot count</h2>
<p>Tournaments are stroke play &mdash; total strokes win, no head-to-head elimination &mdash; usually four rounds with a <b>cut</b> after two that eliminates the bottom half: a league across four days with a knockout filter, the hybrid logic the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats guide</a> sets out. (Match play &mdash; hole-by-hole head-to-head &mdash; survives in events like the Ryder Cup, a team knockout where the format changes everything.) And because scoring is cumulative with no clock, golf's pressure is unique: there is no running out the game, no possession to hold &mdash; only the next shot, and the mathematical certainty that every stroke will appear on the card.</p>
<p>The honest summary for newcomers: watch the leaderboard's red and black numbers &mdash; under par and over &mdash; and the tournament resolves into a running argument with a benchmark. Golf is the only sport where the course itself is the constant opponent, the handicap lets everyone argue with it, and the scorecard keeps receipts.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 par (score)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Par_(score)"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 golf handicap", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golf_handicap")],
[("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work"), ("tennis-scoring-explained", "Tennis scoring, explained")]),

("boxing-scoring-explained", "Boxing scoring, explained: the ten-point must and the judges problem",
"How boxing rounds are scored, why knockdowns dominate the maths, the difference between the belts \u2014 and why the judges are the sport's most contested officials.",
"""<p>Boxing's scoring looks archaic &mdash; three humans with scorecards &mdash; and its controversies are structural rather than accidental. Understanding the system explains both why most decisions are defensible and why the bad ones enrage entire arenas.</p>
<h2>The ten-point must system</h2>
<p>Three ringside judges score each round independently, and the rule is a forced hierarchy: the round winner receives <b>ten points</b>, the loser <b>nine or fewer</b> &mdash; hence "ten-point must." A normal round is 10&ndash;9. A <b>knockdown</b> typically makes it 10&ndash;8 (an extra point per knockdown); dominance without a knockdown can also earn 10&ndash;8, and an even round can be scored 10&ndash;10, though judges are discouraged from it. Fouls deduct points on the referee's instruction. After the scheduled rounds, totals decide: <b>unanimous</b> (all three judges for the same fighter), <b>split</b> (two against one), <b>majority</b>, or a <b>draw</b>. A knockout or referee stoppage overrides all scorecards instantly.</p>
<h2>What the judges are actually told to weigh</h2>
<p>The official criteria run in order: <b>clean punching</b> first, then effective aggression, ring generalship and defence. In practice the ordering does heavy lifting &mdash; volume and activity look impressive but score less than landed, unreturned shots, and a knockdown usually outweighs a dominated round. This is where controversy breeds: a fighter who controls distance and lands cleaner but less visibly can lose rounds to a busier opponent in the eyes of a judge who weighs the wrong criterion. Unlike tennis, where the scoring is arithmetic and public &mdash; the <a href="/sports/tennis-scoring-explained/">tennis explainer</a> shows how transparent systems behave &mdash; boxing keeps scorecards secret until the end, so disagreement arrives as one undebatable verdict rather than a running argument.</p>
<h2>Belts, sanctions and the fragmentation problem</h2>
<p>There is no single governing body crowning one champion per weight: four major sanctioning organisations (WBA, WBC, IBF, WBO) each award belts, so a division can carry four "world champions" simultaneously, with <b>unification</b> bouts &mdash; holders fighting holders &mdash; as the route to an undisputed champion. The fragmentation is commercial as much as sporting: more belts means more title fights means more revenue, and critics have said so for decades. What decides greatness is therefore not the sanctioning letters but the record itself &mdash; who was beaten, when, and how the rounds were scored.</p>
<p>The honest summary: boxing scoring is a subjective ranking of three-minute arguments, run by three separate judges, with a forced winner each round and knockdowns as the loudest evidence. The system produces defensible verdicts most nights and ugly ones often enough to matter &mdash; which is exactly why the sport debates its own judges more than any other debates its officials.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 10-point must system", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/10-point_must_system"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 professional boxing", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professional_boxing")],
[("tennis-scoring-explained", "Tennis scoring, explained"), ("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work")]),

])


SPORT_EXPLAINERS.extend([

("how-ufc-and-mma-work", "How the UFC and MMA work: the sport behind the spectacle",
"Mixed martial arts in plain terms: the unified rules, weight classes, judging and why the UFC is a promotion rather than a league.",
"""<p>Mixed martial arts looks like chaos to the uninitiated and is actually one of the most tightly rule-bounded combat sports &mdash; and its biggest organisation is structured unlike any other in sport. Two things unlock it: the rules that govern the fight, and the business that governs the fights.</p>
<h2>The unified rules</h2>
<p>Modern MMA runs on the <b>unified rules</b> adopted across athletic commissions: bouts of three five-minute rounds (title and main events get five), contested in a cage or ring under a referee with the power to stop the contest at any moment. Victory arrives by <b>knockout</b>, <b>submission</b> (tap or referee stoppage), <b>decision</b> (the judges' scorecards, using the same <a href="/sports/boxing-scoring-explained/">ten-point must system as boxing</a> with effective striking and grappling weighted first), or disqualification. The foul list is specific &mdash; no strikes to the back of the head or spine, no eye-gouging, no soccer kicks to a downed opponent's head &mdash; and the "ground and pound" that shocks newcomers is heavily regulated: a downed fighter is protected far more than the footage suggests.</p>
<h2>Why it is called mixed</h2>
<p>The sport's founding idea was a rules-light tournament between styles &mdash; boxer against wrestler against karateka. The answer the sport discovered, and the reason it now trains as one discipline: everything works, so everything is needed. Modern fighters blend <b>striking</b> (boxing, muay thai, kickboxing), <b>wrestling</b> (which decides where the fight takes place), and <b>submission grappling</b> (which ends it on the ground) &mdash; the family tree the <a href="/sports/grappling-sports-explained/">grappling explainer</a> maps properly. Weight classes from flyweight up to heavyweight keep contests fair on size, and the weigh-in the day before is its own drama because fighters cut weight aggressively to compete lighter than they walk around.</p>
<h2>The UFC is a promotion, not a league</h2>
<p>The structural quirk: the UFC is a <b>promotional company</b>, not a federation or league. There is no single governing body crowning champions of MMA &mdash; the UFC, PFL, ONE and others each run their own rosters, titles and rankings, with the UFC's commercial dominance making its belts the de facto world titles. Fighters are contracted per-organisation (exclusivity being the UFC's norm), which means cross-promotion "superfights" are contract negotiations rather than fixtures &mdash; the opposite of football, where the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">tournament structure</a> decides who meets whom. Rankings exist but the title picture is made by matchmakers, and belts are defended on the promotion's schedule.</p>
<p>The honest newcomer's guide: watch a title fight at a major event and the sport's logic assembles itself &mdash; the feel-out round, the threat that changes everything, the chess of cage position. It is a sport of specialists who had to become generalists, run by a company that had to become an institution, and the tension between those two facts is most of its drama.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 mixed martial arts", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixed_martial_arts"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Ultimate Fighting Championship", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultimate_Fighting_Championship")],
[("boxing-scoring-explained", "Boxing scoring, explained"), ("grappling-sports-explained", "Grappling sports, explained")]),

("grappling-sports-explained", "Grappling sports, explained: judo, wrestling, BJJ and the family tree",
"The ground game decoded: how judo, freestyle wrestling and Brazilian jiu-jitsu differ in goal, scoring and philosophy \u2014 and how they feed MMA.",
"""<p>Grappling &mdash; fighting without striking, decided by throws, holds and submissions &mdash; is the oldest competitive sport there is and now three distinct sports with a shared vocabulary. Understanding the family tree explains both each sport's character and why modern MMA looks the way it does.</p>
<h2>The three main branches</h2>
<p><b>Judo</b>, founded in Japan in the 1880s, is the throwing art: the goal is to throw an opponent decisively onto their back &mdash; a perfect throw scores ippon and wins instantly &mdash; with groundwork as a secondary path. Its philosophy is written into its technique: maximum efficiency, using the opponent's momentum, and the gi (grip-heavy jacket) defines the entire technical system. <b>Freestyle and Greco-Roman wrestling</b>, the Olympic sports, pursue control and exposure: pins end matches, and points reward takedowns, reversals and turning an opponent's shoulders toward the mat &mdash; Greco-Roman bans attacks below the waist entirely, which is why it looks like an upper-body chess match. <b>Brazilian jiu-jitsu</b>, descended from judo via the Gracie family, inverted the hierarchy: the ground is the destination, not a mishap, and the fight is won by submission &mdash; chokes and joint locks &mdash; with the guard (fighting off your back) as a complete offensive system rather than a defensive failure.</p>
<h2>Why they score differently</h2>
<p>Each sport's rules express its theory of fighting. Judo scores the <b>decisive moment</b> &mdash; one clean throw ends it. Wrestling scores <b>control and activity</b> &mdash; accumulating points, with passivity penalised. BJJ scores <b>positional dominance</b> as a ladder toward submission &mdash; points for passing the guard, mounting, taking the back, all proxies for "this position finishes fights." The differences are philosophical, and they produce genuinely different athletes: judoka are explosive grip-fighters, wrestlers are pressure machines with legendary conditioning, jiu-jitsu players are patient problem-solvers comfortable in positions the other two treat as emergencies.</p>
<h2>The family tree in MMA</h2>
<p>The founding question of <a href="/sports/how-ufc-and-mma-work/">modern MMA</a> &mdash; which style wins &mdash; was answered in its first decade: wrestling decided <em>where</em> fights happened, and jiu-jitsu decided <em>how they ended</em> on the ground, while strikers adapted or lost. Every elite mixed martial artist now carries at least one grappling base plus defensive competence in the others, which is why the sport's gyms teach a hybrid no single traditional sport would recognise. Outside MMA the branches thrive separately: judo and wrestling are Olympic constants &mdash; the <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> covers their place in the programme &mdash; and BJJ runs its own tournament ecosystem, with submission-only events stripping the points entirely.</p>
<p>The honest summary for a newcomer choosing a door: judo for the spectacular throws and the strictest tradition, wrestling for the hardest competition and conditioning in sport, BJJ for the deepest tactical rabbit hole and the gentlest entry point for adults starting late. All three teach the same core truth &mdash; that controlling a human body is a technical skill with a century of accumulated answers &mdash; and disagree, productively, about what counts as winning.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 judo", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judo"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Brazilian jiu-jitsu", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_jiu-jitsu")],
[("how-ufc-and-mma-work", "How the UFC and MMA work"), ("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work")]),

("cycling-race-formats-explained", "Cycling race formats, explained: the team sport disguised as individual",
"Road racing's secret: why the winner has a team, what the peloton and breakaways actually are, and how one-day races differ from stage races.",
"""<p>Professional road cycling presents itself as a lone winner crossing a line, and is actually the most intensely team-dependent sport there is &mdash; a rolling physics problem where eight riders spend the race making one of them able to win. Once that inversion clicks, every race becomes legible.</p>
<h2>The physics that creates the tactics</h2>
<p>At racing speed, most of a cyclist's energy fights air resistance &mdash; and riding directly behind another rider saves roughly a third of it. This single fact generates the sport. The <b>peloton</b> is not a crowd but an energy cartel: riders cooperate to share the wind, because the whole group moves faster together than alone. A <b>breakaway</b> is a small group that leaves the cartel and gambles that cooperation plus a head start beats the peloton's superior numbers &mdash; most breakaways are caught, with mathematical predictability that commentators calculate live. <b>Domestiques</b> &mdash; literally "servants" &mdash; are teammates who ride in the wind, fetch bottles and chase breaks so their leader spends the day sheltered until the decisive moment. Cycling is a sport where nearly every entrant knows before the start that their job is to lose so a teammate can win.</p>
<h2>One-day races versus stage races</h2>
<p><b>One-day classics</b> are single brutal events &mdash; the cobbled Monuments of Flanders and Paris&ndash;Roubaix, the climbs of Li&egrave;ge &mdash; decided by timing an attack against riders equally exhausted. <b>Stage races</b> run consecutive daily races whose times aggregate into a general classification, the overall winner being whoever needs the least total time: a league across days, in the vocabulary of the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats guide</a>. The <b>Grand Tours</b> &mdash; three weeks, twenty-one stages &mdash; are the format's Everest: the Tour de France gets its own <a href="/sports/how-the-tour-de-france-works/">explainer</a>, with the Giro d'Italia and Vuelta a Espa&ntilde;a completing the trio. Points and mountain classifications run alongside the time race, letting sprinters and climbers contest their own prizes daily &mdash; multiple competitions inside one race.</p>
<h2>How a stage actually unfolds</h2>
<p>The standard day has a script: a breakaway forms early, the peloton lets it take a calculated margin, sprinters' and leaders' teams control the chase, and the gap falls on schedule until the finale &mdash; a bunch sprint decided at seventy kilometres an hour by lead-out trains, or a climb where the general-classification riders attack each other directly. Time trials are the exception: alone against the clock, no drafting, no team &mdash; the pure test the rest of the format conspires to avoid. The honest viewer's reward: once you watch for the wind rather than the wheels, cycling reads as what it is &mdash; chess played at forty kilometres an hour, where every move is a calculation about energy, and the winner is simply the last rider whose team still has anyone left to spend.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Grand Tour (cycling)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Tour_(cycling)"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 bicycle racing", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bicycle_racing")],
[("how-the-tour-de-france-works", "How the Tour de France works"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("how-the-tour-de-france-works", "How the Tour de France works: three weeks, four jerseys, one team sport",
"The yellow, green, polka-dot and white jerseys decoded, plus the stages, the teams and why the winner rarely wins a single stage.",
"""<p>The Tour de France is the largest annual sporting event on earth &mdash; three weeks, twenty-one stages, roughly 3,500 kilometres, and an audience measured in billions cumulatively. It is also the most misunderstood: the rider in the famous yellow jersey is usually not the one winning the stages, and the race is running at least four competitions at once.</p>
<h2>The jerseys: four races in one</h2>
<p>The <b>maillot jaune</b> (yellow) goes to the leader of the <b>general classification</b> &mdash; lowest cumulative time across all stages. The GC winner is a climber-and-time-trialler who loses almost nothing anywhere; they may not win a single stage, because winning is expensive and consistency is the actual contest. The <b>maillot vert</b> (green) rewards the points classification &mdash; stage finishes and intermediate sprints &mdash; and is the sprinters' jersey. The <b>polka dot</b> goes to the best climber, scored over categorised climbs. The <b>white</b> is the young-rider classification: the GC race for under-26s. Every stage advances all four, which is why breakaways full of riders with no GC hopes are chased hard &mdash; they are contesting somebody's jersey.</p>
<h2>The stage anatomy</h2>
<p>A Tour route is deliberately composed: flat stages for the sprinters, hilly stages for the punchy, high-mountain stages in the Alps and Pyrenees where the GC is decided, and <b>time trials</b> &mdash; alone against the clock &mdash; where pure power separates the contenders. The final stage into Paris is traditionally a bunch sprint and a celebration; the GC is effectively settled before it. Each day follows the script the <a href="/sports/cycling-race-formats-explained/">race-formats explainer</a> sets out: breakaway, controlled chase, finale &mdash; but across three weeks the deeper contest is attrition. Riders burn thousands of calories a day for twenty-one days; the Tour is famously lost in the third week, when accumulated fatigue turns small gaps into decisive ones.</p>
<h2>The teams nobody watches</h2>
<p>Each of the roughly twenty teams fields eight riders built around one or two leaders, and the sport's central inversion lives here: a Tour winner is delivered by seven teammates who will never wear yellow &mdash; domestiques who block wind, chase breaks, surrender their own bikes after crashes and ride mountains at threshold so their leader can follow wheels. Teams also race against each other in the <b>team classification</b>, and alliances of convenience form and dissolve daily. The <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> covers a four-year cycle; the Tour compresses an entire season's meaning into July, which is why riders plan whole careers around peaking for three weeks.</p>
<p>The honest summary: the Tour is a rolling three-week tournament &mdash; daily knockout drama inside a cumulative league &mdash; won by the rider whose team protects them best, whose body survives longest, and who loses the least time on the days that suit them least. Watch one mountain stage with the jerseys explained and the sport's beautiful strangeness announces itself: the winner crosses the line alone, and did not get there alone for a single kilometre.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Tour de France", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tour_de_France"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Grand Tour (cycling)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Tour_(cycling)")],
[("cycling-race-formats-explained", "Cycling race formats, explained"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

("how-snooker-scoring-works", "How snooker scoring works: the colours, the breaks and the safety war",
"Red-and-colours alternation, break building, the respot rules and the frames-and-sessions structure \u2014 snooker's scoring decoded.",
"""<p>Snooker looks deceptively simple from a sofa &mdash; pot balls, score points &mdash; and is actually a scoring system with alternating phases, a built-in comeback mechanism, and a strategic layer most viewers never hear explained. Decode the scoring and the chess appears.</p>
<h2>The red-and-colours engine</h2>
<p>A snooker table starts with fifteen reds (one point each) and six colours &mdash; yellow 2, green 3, brown 4, blue 5, pink 6, black 7 &mdash; on their spots, for a maximum frame total of 147. The alternation rule drives everything: a player must pot a <b>red, then a colour, then a red, then a colour</b>. Each potted colour is respotted until the reds are gone; then the colours are potted in ascending order and stay down. The scoring optimum is obvious once stated &mdash; every red followed by the black is worth eight, so a perfect break runs red-black fifteen times then clears the colours for <b>147, the maximum break</b>, one of sport's rarest achievements. Miss, and the turn passes: the opponent takes the table from wherever the cue ball lies.</p>
<h2>Frames, sessions and the comeback mechanism</h2>
<p>A <b>frame</b> is won by the higher score once the balls are exhausted (or conceded when the remaining points cannot mathematically overtake &mdash; players concede frames routinely, which confuses newcomers). Matches run as races to a frame count: best-of-19 at ranking events, with the World Championship final a best-of-35 spread over four sessions across two days &mdash; an endurance contest as much as a skill one. The built-in drama is <b>snookers and foul points</b>: fouls (missing the ball, hitting the wrong colour, touching a ball) concede a minimum of four points, and when a player is behind with too few points on the table to catch up by potting alone, they can still win by <b>laying snookers</b> &mdash; leaving the cue ball behind a blocking ball so the opponent fouls. The phrase "on the snooker needing snookers" describes sport's tensest arithmetic: a comeback assembled entirely from the opponent's mistakes.</p>
<h2>The strategic layer</h2>
<p>Two skills decide frames beyond potting. <b>Break-building</b>: cue-ball control that positions the white for the next pot &mdash; a century break (100+ in one visit) is the mark of a elite scorer, and the top players' consistency at it is what separates eras. <b>Safety play</b>: deliberate shots that leave the opponent no pot, trading position rather than points &mdash; the long safety exchanges that newcomers call slow are actually the frame's middle game, with every ball a negotiation. The <a href="/sports/golf-scoring-explained/">golf explainer</a> covers a similar benchmark-versus-opponent structure; snooker is unique in that the scoreboard moves only through your own success &mdash; or the opponent's failure &mdash; and never through luck.</p>
<p>The honest summary: snooker is a game of visit management. Pot-and-alternate builds the score, respotting keeps the table rich until the endgame, fouls arm the trailing player, and safety turns dead frames into live ones. Watch one full frame knowing those four mechanics and the sport stops being people potting balls &mdash; it becomes two players arguing, in angles, about who deserves the table.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 snooker", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snooker"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 maximum break", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximum_break")],
[("golf-scoring-explained", "Golf scoring, explained"), ("boxing-scoring-explained", "Boxing scoring, explained")]),

("pro-wrestling-explained", "Pro wrestling, explained: the athletic theatre that outlived its secret",
"Kayfabe, the work and the shoot: how professional wrestling became the world's most successful performance sport \u2014 and why 'it's fake' misses the point.",
"""<p>Professional wrestling is the most-watched live performance art on earth and the most stubbornly misunderstood: dismissed as fake by people who have never watched a match, defended with the wrong arguments by fans. The honest frame is older than the criticism: wrestling is not a sport with a secret, it is a performance with athletics &mdash; and it is worth understanding on its own terms.</p>
<h2>Kayfabe, work and shoot</h2>
<p>The vocabulary matters because the art form runs on it. <b>Kayfabe</b> is the presented reality &mdash; the storylines, the rivalries, the championships treated as competitive achievements. A <b>work</b> is anything scripted or performed; a <b>shoot</b> is anything real, and the art's signature move is blurring the line &mdash; storylines built from performers' actual careers and grievances, worked-shoot promos that leave the audience genuinely unsure. For most of the twentieth century kayfabe was protected absolutely; since the industry's structural exposure in the 1990s, wrestling has done something no other performance medium has managed: it kept telling stories that work <em>knowing</em> the audience knows. The contract is now explicit and unbroken &mdash; audiences consent to the fiction the way they do at the theatre, and the performers never stop executing it with full athletic commitment.</p>
<h2>The athletics are not the fiction</h2>
<p>What is not scripted is the physical execution, and this is where "fake" collapses. Moves are cooperatively performed but genuinely dangerous: bodies take real impact nightly, injuries are occupational and frequent, and the craft skill &mdash; protecting an opponent while making contact read as devastating &mdash; is a physical discipline comparable to stunt performance with the added demand of doing it live, in character, to a crowd whose reaction rewrites the plan in real time. Matches are structured like songs rather than contests: beats and near-falls building to a finish, with crowd response conducting the tempo. The best performers are simultaneously athletes, actors and improvisers &mdash; and the career length reflects all three demands.</p>
<h2>The business underneath</h2>
<p>The structural facts explain the industry's shape. Wrestling is promotion-driven like <a href="/sports/how-ufc-and-mma-work/">MMA</a> &mdash; companies run their own rosters, titles and storylines with no single governing body, WWE's global dominance making its championships the de facto world titles. The product is built for television and now streaming: weekly episodic shows advance the storylines that major events pay off, a serial structure unique among sports-adjacent entertainment. The championship belts are story devices as much as prizes &mdash; title reigns are booked around a performer's push, and the audience's fluency in this is part of the culture. Historically the industry also invented the territory system, the wrestling school pipeline and the modern spectacle entrance, exporting its grammar into MMA presentation itself.</p>
<p>The honest summary: professional wrestling is athletic theatre with a century of accumulated craft, performing the one story sports tells naturally &mdash; the rise, the fall, the comeback &mdash; on a schedule, with authors. Calling it fake is like calling a stunt film fake: the falls are real, the story is chosen, and the skill is in making you feel both at once. The right question is not whether the outcome is fixed. It is whether the performers can make a fixed outcome feel like the most important thing happening tonight &mdash; and on the best nights, they do.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 professional wrestling", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professional_wrestling"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 kayfabe", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kayfabe")],
[("how-ufc-and-mma-work", "How the UFC and MMA work"), ("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work")]),

])


SPORT_EXPLAINERS.extend([

("how-olympic-judging-works", "How Olympic judging works: the sports where humans hold the scoreboard",
"Gymnastics, diving and beyond: how subjective scoring is tamed with codes of points, difficulty values, trimmed panels and the politics that leaks in anyway.",
"""<p>Some Olympic sports are settled by clocks and tape measures; others are settled by people with scorecards. The judged sports are the Games' most misunderstood &mdash; accused of subjectivity while running on some of the most elaborate scoring machinery in sport. Here is how it actually works.</p>
<h2>The modern architecture: two panels, one score</h2>
<p>The old perfect-ten era died because a single blended score could not separate how hard a routine was from how well it was performed. Modern gymnastics answered with the open-ended <b>Code of Points</b>: a <b>D-score</b> (difficulty) that simply adds the declared value of every skill performed &mdash; unbounded, arithmetic, objective once the skills are recognised &mdash; and an <b>E-score</b> (execution) that starts at ten and subtracts for every flaw, judged by a panel. Total is difficulty plus execution: a harder routine with small errors can beat a flawless easy one, which is the trade the sport deliberately chose. Diving runs a parallel system: each dive carries a published tariff, and judges score execution &mdash; with the crucial refinement that the <b>highest and lowest scores are discarded</b> before averaging, the oldest anti-bias device in judging.</p>
<h2>The anti-bias engineering</h2>
<p>Because human judges are human, the systems stack defences: large panels so no single vote decides; trimmed means that discard outliers; separate panels for difficulty and execution so nobody controls both halves; publication of every judge's individual marks, which makes national-favouritism patterns visible and sanctionable &mdash; judging misconduct has ended careers. None of it eliminates the sport's honest residue: a routine on the edge of recognition gets judged in context, reputations shade first impressions, and an Olympic final is judged by people who know exactly what the score means. The scandal version of this &mdash; traded favours between federations &mdash; has happened and been punished; the quiet version is what trimming and transparency exist to squeeze.</p>
<h2>Why judging survives at all</h2>
<p>The alternative &mdash; instrumenting everything &mdash; fails on the thing being measured. Sensors can measure rotation degrees, but artistry, amplitude and composition are judgments, and the sports want them judged. The <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> covers how the Games balance measured and judged sports across the programme; combat sports sit between the two, which the <a href="/sports/boxing-scoring-explained/">ten-point must explainer</a> documents &mdash; the same trim-the-panel logic applied to rounds. The athlete's honest perspective: in a judged sport you compete against the field <em>and</em> the code &mdash; building routines that maximise difficulty the judges will credit, performed cleanly enough that execution deductions stay small. That dual optimisation is the sport within the sport.</p>
<p>The honest summary: Olympic judging is not vibes with flags &mdash; it is arithmetic scaffolding around an irreducibly human judgment, defended by trimmed panels, split duties and published marks. It will never be a stopwatch, and it does not try to be; it tries to be fair in the places fairness can be engineered, and honest about the rest.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 artistic gymnastics", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artistic_gymnastics")],
[("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work"), ("boxing-scoring-explained", "Boxing scoring, explained")]),

("olympic-swimming-explained", "Olympic swimming, explained: heats to finals in a sport decided by hundredths",
"The Olympic pool decoded: event structure from heats to finals, the touchpad finish, strokes and medleys, and why hundredths of a second are trusted.",
"""<p>Olympic swimming is the Games' purest race sport &mdash; no judging, no tactics against teammates, just eight lanes and a clock that reads to the hundredth. And yet almost everything around the racing is structure worth understanding, because the Olympic format asks swimmers to do something no other championship does: win three races in one event in under two days.</p>
<h2>Heats, semis, finals</h2>
<p>Every individual event runs as a tournament of time. <b>Heats</b> in the morning seed the field &mdash; swimmers are ranked across all heats, not just their own, and the fastest advance. <b>Semifinals</b> (in the 50/100/200 events) cut to the eight-lane final; distance events go straight from heats to final. Then the <b>final</b>, usually the next evening. The structure creates the sport's signature tension: world-class swimmers must manage effort across rounds, because emptying the tank in a morning heat can cost the evening medal &mdash; the <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> covers why the Games compress so much into so few days, and nowhere is the compression crueler than the pool. Relays add team selection as a second competition: who swims the heat and who swims the final is itself medal politics.</p>
<h2>The hundredth, and the touchpad's authority</h2>
<p>Races are timed by <b>touchpads</b> &mdash; pressure plates in the wall that the swimmer must physically strike. The clock stops at contact, not at the eye's estimate, and the result stands on the pad even when television replays look otherwise: the finish is the touch, and a glide-in loses to a lunge-in every time. Times read to the hundredth of a second &mdash; and famously no further: after a 2008 race separated first and second by a hundredth with the pool's length tolerance admitting centimetres of variation, timing to the thousandth was abandoned for fairness. A tie at the hundredth means two silvers, and the sport accepts that as honest uncertainty rather than false precision. The strokes divide the programme &mdash; freestyle, backstroke, breaststroke, butterfly &mdash; with the <b>individual medley</b> swimming all four in one race and the <b>marathon</b> taking the sport out of the pool entirely.</p>
<h2>Why the pool is the Olympics' laboratory</h2>
<p>Swimming's margins drive the Games' technological arms race: pool depth and lane-line design to kill waves, suit technology until the record-breaking full-body suits were banned in 2010 &mdash; a rare case of sport reversing its own equipment escalation &mdash; and starting blocks tuned like sprint equipment. The honest viewing guide: watch the turn walls, where races are won in the underwater phase the casual eye skips; watch the lane-four favourite's heat strategy; and watch the touch, because in a sport decided by hundredths, the last movement is the whole argument.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 swimming at the Summer Olympics", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swimming_at_the_Summer_Olympics"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games")],
[("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work"), ("athletics-track-and-field-explained", "Athletics, explained")]),

("olympic-qualifying-explained", "Olympic qualifying, explained: the two-year race before the race",
"How athletes actually reach the Games: qualifying standards, world rankings, quota places, continental slots and universality \u2014 the system behind every opening ceremony.",
"""<p>The opening ceremony shows athletes at the peak of a journey, and almost nothing of the journey itself: for most sports, reaching the Games is a two-year qualifying campaign with its own rules, quotas and heartbreaks. The system looks chaotic from outside and is actually a careful three-part machine.</p>
<h2>The three currencies of qualification</h2>
<p>Almost every sport qualifies athletes through some mix of: <b>standards</b> &mdash; hitting a published performance mark inside the qualifying window (athletics and swimming run entry times/marks, and a standard is an entry, not a guarantee); <b>ranking</b> &mdash; world-ranking points accumulated across designated events over the window, which turns a whole season into one long qualifier and rewards consistency over a single peak; and <b>tournament qualification</b> &mdash; team sports and combat sports earning berths at world championships or continental qualifiers, where the berth belongs to the nation and the nation then picks the athlete. Which mix applies is set sport by sport by each international federation, within an overall cap: the Games has a fixed athlete ceiling, and every sport's quota is negotiated inside it.</p>
<h2>The politics built into the quotas</h2>
<p>Two devices shape the field beyond raw merit. <b>Continental representation places</b> reserve slots per continent in most sports, so every region brings athletes even where one continent dominates the standard lists &mdash; the same inclusion logic that runs the <a href="/sports/paralympics-explained/">Paralympic classification system</a>'s edges. And <b>universality places</b> invite nations with few or no qualified athletes to send a small team, which is why the parade of nations has nearly every country on earth in it &mdash; a deliberate Olympic commitment that the Games would rather field a slower heat than a smaller world. Host nations receive automatic places in team events, and the <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> covers the wider machinery these quotas plug into.</p>
<h2>The human arithmetic</h2>
<p>The system's cruelty is its calendar: a two-year window means injuries land at the worst possible moment, a missed standard by a hundredth ends a four-year cycle, and national selections &mdash; where a country has more qualified athletes than quota places &mdash; stage their own internal Olympics weeks before the real one. The honest summary: qualification is the Games' hidden sport &mdash; standards test the peak, rankings test the season, tournaments test the moment, and the quota system bends the result toward a world stage rather than a leaderboard. Every athlete in your screen's opening ceremony won something you never saw.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic weightlifting", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_weightlifting")],
[("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work"), ("paralympics-explained", "The Paralympics, explained")]),

("olympic-weightlifting-explained", "Olympic weightlifting, explained: two lifts, three attempts, total clarity",
"The oldest Olympic strength sport decoded: snatch and clean and jerk, the attempt system, weight classes and why it is the most dramatic two seconds in sport.",
"""<p>Olympic weightlifting is the Games' most compact drama: one athlete, one bar, two lifts, and a scoreboard so simple it can be read at a glance &mdash; yet behind that simplicity sits one of the most technically demanding skills in sport. It is also, not coincidentally, the direct ancestor of the gym's most loaded exercises.</p>
<h2>The two lifts</h2>
<p>The <b>snatch</b> moves the bar from floor to overhead in one continuous motion &mdash; the most explosive full-body movement in sport, with the bar travelling faster than most people can track and the athlete dropping under it into a full squat catch. The <b>clean and jerk</b> is two-phase: the bar to the shoulders (the clean), then from shoulders to overhead (the jerk), allowing heavier loads because the movement is split. Judges award each attempt with white or red lights against a technical standard &mdash; the lift must finish controlled, elbows locked, feet in line, the descent stable. The competition score is the <b>total</b>: best successful snatch plus best successful clean and jerk, ranked within weight class. No cumulative fatigue scoring, no subjectivity about effort &mdash; the heaviest total wins, full stop.</p>
<h2>The attempt system, and its cruelty</h2>
<p>Each lifter gets <b>three attempts per lift</b>, and attempts are declared in advance and can be raised but never lowered &mdash; which turns the competition into a game of chicken as much as strength. Weights are called in ascending order, so leading lifters attempt last and can watch the field's ceiling before choosing; a lifter who bombs out (three failed attempts in either lift) scores nothing regardless of training-world strength. The bar rises relentlessly, attempts burn irreplaceably, and the last round of a close class is pure nerve economics. Weight classes structure the field &mdash; the same tiering logic every combat sport uses, as the <a href="/sports/how-ufc-and-mma-work/">MMA explainer</a> covers from the other side of the weight cut &mdash; and world records belong to classes, which is why the record books are a matrix rather than a list.</p>
<h2>Why the gym stole its moves</h2>
<p>The competitive lifts and their derivatives &mdash; cleans, snatches, jerks and the pulls beneath them &mdash; became the backbone of athletic strength training because they train exactly what field sports need: explosive triple extension under load. Which makes the sport's honest distinction worth stating: gym lifting borrows the movements; the Olympic platform keeps the contest. The <a href="/sports/how-the-olympics-work-explained/">Olympics explainer</a> places it as the oldest strength sport continuously on the programme, and its drama is unchanged by a century: two lifts, three tries, heaviest total. Everything else is noise around a barbell.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic weightlifting", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_weightlifting"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games")],
[("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work"), ("how-ufc-and-mma-work", "How the UFC and MMA work")]),

("olympic-football-explained", "Olympic football, explained: the World Cup's strange younger sibling",
"Why Olympic football is an under-23 tournament with three exceptions, how the women's tournament differs, and the honour that keeps clubs grudging.",
"""<p>Football at the Olympics is the sport's odd younger sibling: the world's biggest game, played at the world's biggest event, under rules that exist nowhere else. The explanation is a century of institutional chess between the IOC and FIFA &mdash; and the result is genuinely worth understanding before the next tournament.</p>
<h2>The age rule, and its history</h2>
<p>The men's Olympic tournament is an <b>under-23 competition with three over-age players per squad</b>. The rule's origin is protection: FIFA guards the World Cup as football's singular summit, and an unrestricted Olympic tournament would compete with it directly &mdash; so the men's event was deliberately made a youth showcase with a sprinkling of senior stars. The three over-age slots (added in the 1990s) were the negotiated compromise: enough star power to matter, not enough to threaten. Which produces the tournament's strange texture &mdash; future superstars on display years before their World Cups, plus three famous names per squad carrying the team's spine. The <b>women's tournament has no age restriction</b>: it is full senior football, which is why the women's Olympic gold carries the same weight as its World Cup &mdash; an asymmetry that reflects the women's game's different politics, without a century of World Cup protectionism to guard.</p>
<h2>The tournament itself</h2>
<p>The format follows the Olympic template the <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats explainer</a> generalises: group stage then knockout, compressed into the Games' fortnight, played across host-nation venues well beyond the Olympic city. Squad sizes are small against a long tournament, so rotation and the age profile decide as much as talent. And the club conflict is structural: the Olympics fall outside the mandatory-release calendar for clubs (unlike the World Cup), so every squad is a negotiation &mdash; the institutional friction visible in who is missing.</p>
<h2>Why it still matters</h2>
<p>The honest case for Olympic football's importance: for the men's game it is the largest global youth stage there is &mdash; World Cups for this age group have smaller reach &mdash; and history is full of senior careers announced on the Olympic platform. For the women's game it is a full-weight major trophy, alternating with the World Cup as the sport's biggest biennial prize. And for both, it carries the one honour club football cannot sell: a medal, a podium, a national anthem &mdash; the Olympic packaging doing what no club final can. Watch it knowing the rules and you are watching two different sports in matching kits &mdash; one a coming-of-age tournament, one a genuine world title, sharing a fixture list by a century of institutional truce.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 football at the Summer Olympics", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Football_at_the_Summer_Olympics"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games")],
[("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work"), ("how-the-olympics-work-explained", "How the Olympics work")]),

("olympic-basketball-explained", "Olympic basketball, explained: FIBA rules, pro stars and the shortest road to a gold medal",
"The Olympic basketball tournament decoded: how FIBA rules differ from the NBA, the Dream Team legacy, the women's dynasty and a format with no room for a slow start.",
"""<p>Olympic basketball packs the world's most famous players into the world's most unforgiving format: a tournament so short that one bad quarter ends a gold-medal campaign. And it plays by rules that will surprise anyone who learned the game from the NBA.</p>
<h2>FIBA rules, not NBA rules</h2>
<p>The Olympics play under <b>FIBA</b> &mdash; international basketball's governing body &mdash; and the differences are not cosmetic. Quarters are <b>ten minutes</b>, not twelve; the three-point arc is closer; the key is a rectangle, not an hourglass; goaltending applies once the ball touches the rim; and team foul penalties arrive sooner, making foul management a sharper tactical thread. Most consequentially for style: the shorter game compresses runs &mdash; a twelve-point NBA swing is a half-quarter, but a twelve-point Olympic swing is a crisis. The <a href="/sports/how-sport-tournament-formats-work/">formats explainer</a> covers why compressed formats change tactics; basketball is the clearest Olympic proof, and the <a href="/sports/how-the-nba-works/">NBA guide</a> covers the club game's longer rhythms from the other side.</p>
<h2>The format's cruelty</h2>
<p>Twelve teams, groups, then a knockout &mdash; and no best-of series anywhere. The NBA decides its champion over up to twenty-eight games; the Olympics decide theirs over six, with a single-elimination medal round where one cold shooting night against a disciplined European or Australian side ends four years of waiting. Roster construction reflects it: national teams are assembled in weeks from players who spent the season as opponents, so systems beat talent pools more often than club coaches would predict. Since professionals were admitted &mdash; the 1992 American "Dream Team" being the seismic moment &mdash; the men's tournament has been the highest level of international basketball played, while the gap has steadily narrowed: Olympic gold is no longer an American default, and losses are scored in national trauma.</p>
<h2>The women's tournament</h2>
<p>The women's Olympic tournament is the same format with an even more remarkable story at its top: the American women have sustained the longest dynasty in Olympic team sport &mdash; a gold-medal run measured in decades, built on a development pipeline every other nation studies and none has yet replicated. The matches beneath the dynasty are the most competitive in the world, and the tournament functions as the women's game's joint-summit alongside its World Cup.</p>
<p>The honest summary: Olympic basketball is the sport's most concentrated form &mdash; FIBA's tighter rules, a fortnight's calendar, and single-elimination stakes with no series to hide in. The best players in the world, the shortest road to glory, and the least margin for a slow start anywhere in sport. If you watch one Olympic team-sport tournament knowing only one thing, make it this: in a six-game tournament, every quarter is a season.</p>""",
[("Wikipedia \u2014 basketball at the Summer Olympics", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basketball_at_the_Summer_Olympics"),
 ("Wikipedia \u2014 Olympic Games", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games")],
[("how-sport-tournament-formats-work", "How tournament formats work"), ("how-the-nba-works", "How the NBA works")]),

])
